window.LA=[{"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit I - Welfare", "q": "What do you mean by Pareto Optimality? Explain the Pareto optimality criteria (marginal conditions).", "y": "2018, 2022, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Vilfredo Pareto** (Italian economist) gave the criterion of welfare\n- **Meaning:** no one can be made better off without making someone worse off\n- **Pareto improvement:** a change that makes someone better off and nobody worse off\n- **Ordinal utility** is used, no interpersonal comparison needed\n- **Basis of modern welfare economics** and efficiency analysis\n# Assumptions\n- Two consumers, two goods, two factors (2x2x2 model)\n- Perfect competition in all markets\n- Utility is ordinal, given tastes\n- Production functions are known and given\n- Given resources and technology\n- No externalities, no public goods\n- Rational consumers and producers\n# Three Marginal Conditions\n- **1. Exchange optimum (consumption):** MRS of two goods equal for all consumers\n[[MRS(A) xy = MRS(B) xy]]\n- If MRS differ, consumers can gain by trading, so not optimal\n- Shown by **Edgeworth box** of exchange, contract curve is the locus of tangency\n- **2. Production optimum:** MRTS of two factors equal for all producers\n[[MRTS(X) lk = MRTS(Y) lk]]\n- If MRTS differ, shifting factors raises output of one good without lowering the other\n- Shown by Edgeworth box of production, production contract curve\n- **3. Product-mix optimum:** MRS equals MRT (marginal rate of transformation)\n[[MRS xy = MRT xy]]\n- If MRS is not equal to MRT, society can change the output mix to raise welfare\n- MRT is the slope of the production possibility curve\n- Grand utility possibility frontier is the combination of all three conditions\n# Pareto Optimality and Perfect Competition\n- **Perfect competition ensures** Pareto optimality because P = MC = MR\n- Each consumer equates MRS to price ratio, so MRS are equal\n- Each producer equates MRTS to factor price ratio\n- MRT = ratio of MCs = price ratio = MRS\n# Pareto Optimal Position\n- There are many Pareto optimal points, not only one\n- Each point is on the utility possibility curve\n- Social welfare function is needed to select the best point (Bergson, Samuelson)\n# Limitations and Criticism\n- Silent on **income distribution** and equity\n- Ignores **externalities** and public goods\n- Assumes perfect competition, which is unreal\n- No interpersonal comparison, so cannot judge policy that makes some lose\n- Ignores **second best** problem (Lipsey and Lancaster)\n- Status quo bias, depends on initial distribution of resources\n- Static view\n- Value judgements are hidden\n# Importance\n- Foundation of welfare economics (First and Second Fundamental Theorems)\n- Used in cost-benefit analysis and policy\n- Basis for compensation tests of Kaldor and Hicks\n> Conclusion: Pareto optimality is a minimum efficiency test, not a full test of social welfare."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit I - Welfare", "q": "Critically examine the Kaldor-Hicks compensation principle. Is it superior to Pareto criterion?", "y": "2016, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Problem:** Pareto criterion cannot judge changes where some gain and others lose\n- **Solution:** Kaldor (1939) and Hicks (1940) gave compensation tests\n- **Aim:** to make welfare economics useful for policy\n- **Basis:** potential Pareto improvement\n# Kaldor Criterion\n- A change is an improvement if **gainers can compensate losers** and still be better off\n- Compensation is **hypothetical,** not actually paid\n- Based on a comparison of the gains and the losses in money terms\n- Example: new road gives traders Rs 100 gain, farmers lose Rs 60, so improvement\n# Hicks Criterion\n- A change is an improvement if **losers cannot bribe gainers** to stop the change\n- Looks at the change from the viewpoint of losers\n- Both give the same result in many cases\n# Scitovsky Double Criterion\n- **Scitovsky paradox:** Kaldor test may approve a change and also its reversal\n- Scitovsky said both the change and its reversal must be tested\n- Change is an improvement if it passes the Kaldor test and the reverse move fails it\n# Little's Criticism and Other Tests\n- **I. M. D. Little:** added a distribution criterion (distribution should not worsen)\n- **Samuelson:** utility possibility frontier approach, tests are not sufficient\n- **Bergson:** social welfare function is needed\n# Merits\n- Wider than the Pareto criterion\n- Can be used for tax, tariff, public projects\n- Base of **cost-benefit analysis**\n- Avoids interpersonal comparison of utility (in theory)\n- Supports efficiency and growth arguments\n# Demerits\n- **Hypothetical compensation:** if not paid, losers remain worse off\n- **Value judgement** is hidden: assumes a rupee is of equal worth to rich and poor\n- **Scitovsky paradox** shows inconsistency\n- Ignores income distribution\n- Uses money as the measure of welfare, a cardinal view\n- Assumes given prices and tastes\n- Difficult to measure gains and losses\n# Importance\n- Gave a practical test for policy makers\n> Conclusion: Compensation tests widen the Pareto test, but they cannot escape value judgements, so they are only partly superior."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "How is a cartel formed in an oligopoly market? Explain the concept of market-sharing cartel.", "y": "2019, 2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Oligopoly:** few sellers, interdependence, uncertainty about rivals\n- **Cartel:** formal agreement among firms to fix price, output or share the market\n- **Aim:** to avoid competition, act like a monopolist, earn maximum joint profit\n- **Examples:** OPEC, diamond cartel (De Beers)\n# Why Cartels are Formed\n- To reduce uncertainty about rivals\n- To avoid price wars\n- To earn monopoly profit jointly\n- To restrict entry of new firms\n- To bargain with buyers and suppliers\n# Types of Cartel\n- **Perfect (centralised) cartel:** one central body decides all\n- **Market-sharing cartel:** firms agree on price and divide the market\n# Perfect (Centralised) Cartel\n- **Central agency** is set up by the member firms\n- Agency **adds up the marginal costs** of all firms to get industry MC\n- **Industry MR** is derived from market demand curve\n- Equilibrium output where **industry MC = industry MR**\n[[Industry: MC = MR ; Price from demand curve]]\n- Total output is then distributed among firms so that **MC of each firm = industry MR**\n[[MC1 = MC2 = ... = MCn = MR]]\n- **Profit** is also pooled and then shared (by agreement, not necessarily by cost)\n- Low-cost firms produce more\n# Market-Sharing Cartel\n- Firms agree on **a common price,** no central body\n- Share the market by:\n- **Non-price competition:** advertising, quality, service\n- **Quota system:** fixed output share by sales or capacity\n- **Geographical division:** each firm gets an area\n- Each firm sells at the agreed price and its profit depends on its own cost\n- Low-cost firms gain more\n# Instability of Cartels\n- **Incentive to cheat:** a firm can increase output secretly\n- **New entrants** may undercut\n- **Difference in cost** and demand among firms\n- **Legal restrictions** (anti-trust, MRTP, Competition Act, 2002)\n- Government control and changes in demand\n- Disagreement about quota and share\n# Conclusion\n> A cartel reduces competition and acts like a monopoly. It is in the firms' interest but harmful for consumers, and is often unstable."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "What is monopolistic competition? Discuss Chamberlin's model with free entry of firms.", "y": "2018, 2020, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **E. H. Chamberlin** (Theory of Monopolistic Competition, 1933) and **Joan Robinson** (Imperfect Competition, 1933)\n- **Meaning:** market with many sellers, differentiated products, free entry\n- **Mix of monopoly and competition**\n- **Examples:** toothpaste, soaps, restaurants, clothes\n# Features\n- **Large number of sellers** (but not as many as in perfect competition)\n- **Product differentiation:** by brand, quality, packing, location, service\n- **Free entry and exit** in the long run\n- **Selling costs:** advertising and sales promotion\n- **Downward sloping demand curve:** elastic, firm has limited price control\n- **Non-price competition**\n- **Independent decision** of firms (no mutual dependence)\n- **Imperfect knowledge** of consumers\n# Short Run Equilibrium\n- Firm equates **MC = MR**\n- Price is above AC, so it earns **supernormal profit** (or may suffer loss)\n[[SR: MC = MR ; AR above AC]]\n# Long Run Equilibrium\n- Supernormal profit attracts **new firms**\n- Demand curve of each firm shifts to the left and becomes more elastic\n- Equilibrium where **AR curve is tangent to LAC**\n[[LR: MC = MR and AR = AC]]\n- Only **normal profit** is earned\n- If there are losses, firms leave and the opposite happens\n# Excess Capacity\n- Firm produces less than **minimum-AC output** (optimum scale)\n- The gap = **excess capacity**\n- **Price is above MC,** hence allocative inefficiency\n- Cost of product variety\n# Chamberlin's Group Equilibrium\n- **DD curve:** demand when all firms change price\n- **dd curve:** demand when only one firm changes price\n- Group equilibrium needs P = AC for the average firm\n# Selling Costs\n- Shift demand to the right, make it less elastic\n- U-shaped selling cost curve\n# Criticism\n- Group concept unrealistic (uniform demand and cost assumed)\n- Product differentiation makes group heterogeneous (Kaldor)\n- Excess capacity not wasteful if variety is valued (Demsetz)\n- Ignores interdependence in small groups\n- Free entry in the long run is not always true\n# Importance\n- Realistic market form in many sectors\n- Explains advertising and branding\n> Conclusion: Monopolistic competition gives normal profits, excess capacity and variety."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Explain Cournot's duopoly model. How is equilibrium achieved through reaction curves?", "y": "2016, 2017, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Augustin Cournot** (French mathematician), 1838, first oligopoly model\n- **Duopoly:** two sellers of a homogeneous product\n- **Aim:** to find the equilibrium output of the two sellers\n- **Example used:** two firms selling mineral water from springs\n# Assumptions\n- Two firms, **identical product,** same market\n- **Zero cost** of production (or equal costs)\n- **Linear demand curve** for the market\n- Each firm decides its **output, not price**\n- Each firm assumes that **rival's output remains constant** (zero conjectural variation)\n- Firms take decisions simultaneously\n- No entry of other firms\n# Working of the Model\n- Firm A first acts as monopolist and produces **OQ/2** of the market OQ\n- Firm B assumes A's output is fixed and takes half of the remaining: **OQ/4**\n- A now sees B's output and reduces its output to **3/8**\n- B adjusts: output rises (**5/16**)\n- This process of adjustment continues, A's output falls and B's rises\n- Both firms reach equilibrium\n# Equilibrium Output\n[[A = OQ/3 ; B = OQ/3 ; industry = 2OQ/3]]\n- Each firm gets one-third of the market, one-third is unserved\n- Price is higher than competitive price, lower than monopoly price\n# Reaction Curves\n- **Reaction curve of A (RA):** shows A's best output for each output of B\n- Slopes **downwards,** higher B output means lower A output\n- **Reaction curve of B (RB)** is likewise drawn\n- **Cournot equilibrium** is at the intersection of RA and RB\n- At this point neither firm wants to change output\n# Stability\n- If the firms are away from equilibrium, adjustment brings them back\n- So it is **stable equilibrium**\n# General Case\n- With n firms, each produces 1/(n+1) of the market\n- Industry output = n/(n+1) of the competitive output\n- As n rises, result nears perfect competition\n# Criticism\n- Naive behaviour: assumes the rival never changes output\n- **Ignores interdependence** and price strategy (Bertrand)\n- Assumes no cost, no entry, homogeneous product\n- Cannot explain price leadership or collusion\n- Static, one-period model\n# Importance\n- First formal model of oligopoly, base for game theory (Nash)\n> Conclusion: Cournot's model is simple and historic, but its behaviour assumption is unrealistic."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Explain Kinked Demand Curve theory of oligopoly.", "y": "2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Paul Sweezy** (USA) and **Hall and Hitch** (UK), 1939\n- **Aim:** to explain **price rigidity** in oligopoly\n- **Observation:** prices in oligopoly stay stable for long periods\n- **Idea:** demand curve of the firm has a kink at the existing price\n# Assumptions\n- **Few sellers,** product may be differentiated\n- If a firm **cuts price,** rivals also cut it, to retain customers\n- If a firm **raises price,** rivals do not follow\n- Cost conditions are given\n- The existing price is accepted by all\n# The Kinked Demand Curve\n- **Above the kink:** demand is highly **elastic** (price rise loses customers)\n- **Below the kink:** demand is **inelastic** (price cut gives little gain)\n- The two segments join at the **kink**\n- Kink is at existing price P and output Q\n# Marginal Revenue Curve\n- MR curve has **two parts,** one for each segment of demand\n- There is a **discontinuity (gap) in MR** at the output corresponding to kink\n- Marginal cost curve can shift up or down within the gap\n[[Price and output remain unchanged if MC moves within the MR gap]]\n# Result\n- **Price rigidity** even when cost changes (within a limit)\n- If MC rises sharply beyond the gap, price changes\n- Firms try to compete by non-price methods: advertising, quality, service\n# Merits\n- Explains price stability in many oligopoly markets\n- Simple and realistic reaction pattern\n# Criticism\n- **Does not explain how the original price is determined**\n- Not a theory of price, only of rigidity\n- **Stigler** found evidence against: oligopoly prices do change\n- Rivals may follow price rises in a boom\n- Ignores collusion and leadership\n# Importance\n- Useful in explaining why firms do not engage in price wars\n> Conclusion: Kinked demand curve explains stickiness of price, not its level."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit III - Consumption", "q": "Explain the Permanent Income Hypothesis and compare it with the Absolute Income hypothesis.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Milton Friedman,** A Theory of the Consumption Function (1957)\n- **Aim:** to solve the **puzzle** between the short-run and long-run consumption function\n- **Puzzle:** Cross-section data and short run show APC falls, long-run data (Kuznets) show APC constant\n# Main Idea\n- Consumption depends on **permanent income,** not current income\n- **Permanent income:** the average expected income over a long period (lifetime)\n- Consumers plan their spending on the basis of long-term income\n[[Cp = k . Yp]]\n# Components of Measured Income\n- **Y = Yp + Yt**\n- **Yp:** permanent income\n- **Yt:** transitory income (windfall gain or loss)\n- **Consumption = Cp + Ct** (permanent and transitory consumption)\n- Correlation of Yt with Yp is zero, Ct with Cp is zero, Yt with Ct is zero\n# The Factor k\n- Proportion of permanent income consumed\n- Depends on **rate of interest**\n- Depends on **ratio of wealth to income (w)**\n- Depends on **age and tastes (u)**\n- Not on the level of income\n# Short Run\n- A rise in current income is mostly transitory, so it is mostly saved\n- So **short-run MPC is low** and APC falls in the short run\n# Long Run\n- Permanent income changes, so consumption changes in proportion\n- **APC = MPC = k** in the long run\n- The long-run consumption function is a straight line from the origin\n# Cross-section Analysis\n- High-income groups have positive transitory income, so APC looks lower\n- Low-income groups have negative transitory income, so APC looks higher\n# Comparison with Keynes\n- **Keynes:** current income, APC falls with income\n- **Friedman:** permanent income, APC constant in long run\n- **Keynes:** short-run view. **Friedman:** long-run planning\n# Criticism\n- Permanent income cannot be measured directly\n- Assumes consumers have perfect foresight\n- Assumption of zero correlation is debatable\n- Ignores credit and liquidity limits\n- Rational planning is not true for poor\n# Importance\n- Policy effect: temporary tax cuts have small effect on consumption\n- Basis of the life cycle theory (Modigliani)\n> Conclusion: Friedman reconciled the evidence, showing that consumption depends on long-term income."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit III - Consumption", "q": "What are the limitations of Keynesian consumption function? Has Duesenberry given a better approach?", "y": "2017, 2022", "a": "# Keynes's Consumption Function\n- **C = a + bY,** with a as autonomous consumption and b as MPC\n- **Psychological law:** consumption rises with income, but by less than the rise in income\n- **MPC < 1, APC falls** as income rises\n# Limitations of the Keynesian Function\n- **Only current absolute income** is considered\n- **Kuznets (1946):** long-run data show APC constant, against Keynes\n- **Cross-section data** support Keynes but time series do not\n- **Wealth effect ignored** (Pigou, Modigliani)\n- **Interest rate** influence ignored\n- **Past income and habits** ignored\n- **Demonstration effect** and social factors ignored\n- **Expectations and uncertainty** ignored\n- **Distribution of income** is ignored\n- **Credit availability** and price level not considered\n- **Short-run and long-run difference** is not explained\n- **Age structure** of population, urbanisation ignored\n# Post-Keynesian Theories\n- **Duesenberry:** relative income hypothesis\n- **Friedman:** permanent income\n- **Modigliani:** life cycle\n# Duesenberry's Relative Income Hypothesis\n- **Consumption depends on relative income** (income compared with others) and **highest past income**\n- **Demonstration effect:** people imitate those of higher income\n- **Ratchet effect:** consumption does not fall easily when income falls\n- The consumption function is irreversible\n[[Ct/Yt = a + b (Yt/Y0)]]\n# Short Run and Long Run\n- **Short run:** when income falls, consumption falls less, so APC rises\n- **Long run:** with steady growth in income, APC is constant\n- Explains both cross-section and time-series data\n# Criticism of Duesenberry\n- Based on imitation, difficult to measure\n- Ignores planning, rational choice\n- Lack of micro foundations\n- Ratchet effect is not always found\n# Merits\n- Explains the difference in short and long run\n- Social psychology as part of economics\n> Conclusion: Duesenberry's approach is more realistic than Keynes, though not free of weaknesses."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit IV - Money and IS-LM", "q": "What do you mean by IS-LM curve? Discuss how equilibrium is established in IS-LM model.", "y": "2016, 2022, 2023", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Hicks (1937)** and **Hansen** presented the IS-LM model\n- **Aim:** to show the simultaneous equilibrium of the **goods market and money market**\n- It combines **Keynesian and classical views**\n- Determines **equilibrium income (Y) and interest rate (r)**\n# IS Curve (Goods Market)\n- **IS:** combination of r and Y at which **Investment = Saving**\n- **Slope:** downward sloping\n- **Reason:** lower r leads to higher investment, higher income, so Y rises as r falls\n[[I(r) = S(Y)  or  Y = C + I + G]]\n- **Slope depends on** interest elasticity of investment and multiplier\n- Flatter IS if investment is elastic or MPC is high\n# Derivation of IS\n- Investment curve, saving curve and 45-degree line are used\n- At each r, find Y for which I = S\n- Join the points to get IS\n# Shift of IS\n- **Rise in G or fall in taxes** shifts IS right\n- **Rise in autonomous investment** or exports shifts IS right\n- **Fall in G** shifts IS left\n# LM Curve (Money Market)\n- **LM:** combination of r and Y at which **demand for money = supply of money**\n- **Slope:** upward sloping\n- **Reason:** higher Y raises transaction demand, so r must rise to keep money balance\n[[M/P = L(Y, r)]]\n- Three zones: **Keynesian (horizontal),** intermediate, **classical (vertical)**\n# Derivation of LM\n- L = L1 (transaction, precautionary) + L2 (speculative)\n- With fixed M, a rise in Y raises L1, leaving less for L2, so r rises\n# Shift of LM\n- **Rise in money supply** shifts LM right\n- **Rise in price level** shifts LM left\n- **Rise in liquidity preference** shifts LM left\n# Equilibrium\n- At the **point of intersection of IS and LM** both markets are in equilibrium\n- Income is Y* and interest rate is r*\n- **Disequilibrium:** excess saving or excess money supply pushes it back\n# Policy Analysis\n- **Fiscal policy:** expansion shifts IS right, Y and r rise\n- **Crowding out:** higher r reduces private investment\n- **Monetary policy:** expansion shifts LM right, Y rises and r falls\n- **Liquidity trap:** LM horizontal, monetary policy ineffective\n- **Investment trap:** IS vertical, monetary policy ineffective\n# Criticism\n- Static, no price level or inflation\n- Ignores supply side and expectations\n- Money supply is exogenous (assumption)\n- No role of banking or credit\n# Importance\n- Standard tool for macro policy\n> Conclusion: IS-LM shows how real and monetary factors jointly decide income and interest."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit V - Growth and Cycles", "q": "Write a note on Hicks' trade cycle theory. Is it an improvement on Samuelson's theory?", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **J. R. Hicks,** A Contribution to the Theory of the Trade Cycle (1950)\n- **Trade cycle:** wave-like fluctuation of income, output and employment\n- **Built on:** multiplier-accelerator interaction of Samuelson, with a **ceiling and floor**\n# Basic Elements\n- **Autonomous investment (A):** independent of income, grows at a steady rate\n- **Induced investment (I):** depends on change in income (accelerator)\n- **Multiplier:** raises income from investment\n- **Accelerator (v):** investment depends on change in output\n- **Lag:** consumption lags income\n- **Ceiling:** full employment limit\n- **Floor:** lower limit (disinvestment is limited)\n# Phases of the Cycle\n- **Upswing (boom):** autonomous investment raises income, accelerator makes induced investment, multiplier raises income more\n- **Cumulative rise** in income with an explosive path\n- **Ceiling reached:** income cannot rise beyond full employment output, so the boom ends\n- **Downswing (recession):** income falls, induced investment falls to zero\n- **Depression:** income falls along the explosive path till the floor\n- **Floor:** disinvestment is limited by replacement of worn capital, so fall stops\n- **Recovery:** autonomous investment brings income up again\n# Role of Ceiling and Floor\n- Ceiling is set by **resources (full employment)**\n- Floor is set by **autonomous investment and minimum consumption**\n- They turn explosive movement into **bounded cycles**\n# Comparison with Samuelson\n- Samuelson gives damped, explosive and steady paths\n- Explosive path has no turning points in Samuelson\n- Hicks adds a **ceiling and floor**, so the cycle repeats\n# Merits\n- Gives **turning points** of the cycle\n- Combines multiplier, accelerator and growth\n- Realistic cycle of finite amplitude\n# Criticism\n- Ceiling and floor are mechanical and rigid\n- **Accelerator is assumed constant** (unrealistic)\n- Floor assumes full cut in induced investment\n- Ignores money, price changes and expectations\n- Autonomous investment growth is assumed\n> Conclusion: Hicks's model is an improvement on Samuelson's, but it is a mechanical explanation."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Discuss the doctrine of Reciprocal Demand with the help of offer curves.", "y": "2016, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **J. S. Mill** (Principles of Political Economy, 1848) gave the theory of reciprocal demand\n- **Problem:** Ricardo's theory shows only the limits of trade, not the actual terms of trade\n- **Mill's answer:** the terms of trade are set by the **strength and elasticity of each country's demand for the other's product**\n- **Developed by:** Marshall and Edgeworth using **offer curves**\n# Assumptions\n- Two countries, two commodities\n- Free trade, no transport cost\n- Constant cost, full employment\n- Perfect competition, barter exchange\n- Labour is mobile within, not between countries\n# Limits of Terms of Trade (Ricardo-Mill)\n- **Domestic cost ratio of each country** fixes the limits\n- Example: India: 1 cloth = 2 wheat. Britain: 1 cloth = 1 wheat\n- Terms of trade lie **between 1 and 2 wheat per cloth**\n- Outside these limits, trade does not take place\n# Mill's Principle\n- Within the limits, terms are fixed by **reciprocal demand**\n- **Law of equation of international demand:** terms of trade adjust so that value of exports equals value of imports\n- **Greater demand of A for B's good,** worse the terms for A\n- **Gains are greater for** the country whose demand is less intense (less elastic)\n# Offer Curves (Marshall-Edgeworth)\n- **Offer curve:** shows how much of its export a country is ready to give for each quantity of import\n- It is drawn from the origin, curved\n- **Slope of the ray from origin:** terms of trade\n- **Shape depends on elasticity** of reciprocal demand\n# Equilibrium\n- **Intersection of the two offer curves** is the equilibrium\n- The terms of trade at this point equate quantity offered and quantity wanted\n- **At other terms of trade,** one country offers more than the other wants, so terms change\n# Effects of Change in Demand\n- **Rise in country A's demand for B's goods:** A's offer curve shifts, TOT turns against A\n- **Rise in productivity of A:** offer curve of A shifts, TOT can turn against A\n- **Tariff:** shifts the offer curve of the imposing country, can improve its TOT\n# Stability\n- Equilibrium is stable if the offer curves cut in the right way (Marshall-Lerner)\n# Merits\n- Explains the actual terms of trade\n- Brings demand side into trade theory\n- Offer curve is a useful tool\n# Criticism\n- Ignores cost side and factor endowments (Ohlin)\n- Two country, two good model is unreal\n- Constant costs assumed\n- Does not explain the causes of trade\n- Static and ignores capital movement\n> Conclusion: Mill's doctrine completes Ricardo's theory by fixing the terms of trade through reciprocal demand."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit III - Terms of Trade", "q": "What do you mean by terms of trade? Explain its various kinds and importance.", "y": "2016, 2017, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Terms of trade (TOT):** the rate at which one country's exports exchange for its imports\n- **Importance:** shows the share of gains from trade\n- **Concepts:** Taussig, Viner, Dorrance, Prebisch\n# Kinds of Terms of Trade\n- **1. Net barter TOT (commodity TOT):** ratio of export price index to import price index\n[[Net barter TOT = (Px / Pm) x 100]]\n- Rise means improvement, fall means deterioration\n- **2. Gross barter TOT (Taussig):** ratio of quantity of imports to quantity of exports\n[[Gross barter TOT = (Qm / Qx) x 100]]\n- **3. Income TOT (Dorrance):** net barter TOT multiplied by export volume index\n[[Income TOT = (Px / Pm) x Qx]]\n- Shows import capacity from exports\n- **4. Single factoral TOT (Viner):** net barter multiplied by productivity index of exports\n- **5. Double factoral TOT (Viner):** includes productivity of the foreign country's factors also\n- **6. Real cost TOT (Viner):** takes into account real cost (disutility) of labour\n- **7. Utility TOT (Viner):** includes utility of imports and disutility of exports\n# Determinants of TOT\n- Reciprocal demand and elasticity\n- Productivity changes and technology\n- Tariffs, quotas\n- Exchange rate changes\n- Inflation and growth\n- Terms depend on demand for exports and imports\n# Prebisch-Singer Hypothesis\n- **Terms of trade of developing countries decline** in the long run (primary vs manufactured goods)\n- Reasons: low income elasticity of demand for primary goods, monopoly in industrial countries\n- Policy: import substitution, diversification\n# Importance\n- Index of benefits of trade\n- Guides trade policy and tariffs\n- Gives a base for BOP analysis\n- Helps in comparing the growth impact\n- Indicates welfare of a country\n# Limitations\n- Net barter ignores quantity and quality changes\n- Index number problems\n- Does not show total welfare\n- Ignores services and capital flows\n# India's TOT\n- Fluctuations due to oil import prices and export of manufactures\n> Conclusion: TOT is a useful but incomplete indicator of the gain from trade."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Critically evaluate the Purchasing Power Parity theory of exchange rate.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Gustav Cassel** (Swedish economist) gave PPP in **1918** (earlier ideas by Ricardo, Wheatley)\n- **Idea:** exchange rate between two currencies is determined by their relative **purchasing power**\n- **Law of one price** is the base\n# Absolute PPP\n- Exchange rate equals ratio of price levels\n[[R = Pa / Pb]]\n- Example: basket costs Rs 800 in India and $10 in USA, so R = Rs 80 per $\n# Relative PPP\n- Change in exchange rate equals change in relative price levels\n[[R1 = R0 x (Pa1/Pa0) / (Pb1/Pb0)]]\n- If domestic inflation is higher, its currency depreciates\n# Assumptions\n- Free trade, no transport cost, no tariffs\n- Perfect competition, perfect information\n- Price indices are comparable\n- Flexible exchange rate\n- Price level determines exchange rate (not vice versa)\n# Uses\n- Gives the long-run equilibrium exchange rate\n- Used to fix the **new parity** after the war or devaluation (after WWI)\n- Comparison of living standards between countries (GDP at PPP)\n- Test of currency overvaluation (Big Mac index)\n# Criticism\n- **Ignores capital flows,** which affect exchange rates\n- **Non-traded goods** (services, land) are not equalised\n- **Transport costs, tariffs, quotas** break the law of one price\n- **Price index problem:** different goods, weights and quality\n- **Reverse causation:** exchange rate also changes prices\n- **Taste and demand differences**\n- **Expectations and speculation** affect exchange rate\n- **Balassa-Samuelson effect:** price levels are higher in rich countries\n- **Empirical evidence:** weak in short run, better in long run\n- **Government intervention** in exchange markets\n# Merits\n- Simple and clear\n- Useful in long-term analysis\n- Highlights role of inflation\n- Basis for exchange rate policy\n> Conclusion: PPP is a useful long-run guide but cannot fully explain exchange rate behaviour."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "What do you mean by Balance of Payments? Discuss the Absorption approach to correct disequilibrium.", "y": "2016, 2018, 2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Balance of Payments (BOP):** systematic record of all economic transactions between residents of a country and the rest of the world in one year\n- **Based on double-entry book-keeping:** credit (receipts) and debit (payments)\n- **BOP always balances** in accounting sense, but may be in disequilibrium\n# Components of BOP\n- **Current account:** visible trade (goods), invisible trade (services), investment income, unilateral transfers\n- **Capital account:** FDI, portfolio investment, external loans, banking capital\n- **Official reserve account:** changes in foreign exchange reserves, gold, SDR\n- **Errors and omissions:** balancing item\n# Balance of Trade vs BOP\n- BOT: only goods. BOP: all transactions\n- BOT is a part of the current account\n# Equilibrium and Disequilibrium\n- **Autonomous items** (above the line) vs **accommodating items** (below the line)\n- Deficit when autonomous payments exceed autonomous receipts\n# Absorption Approach (Sidney Alexander, 1952)\n- **Aim:** to analyse the effect of devaluation on BOP using income and absorption\n- **Absorption (A):** total domestic expenditure = C + I + G\n- **Identity:** Y = A + (X - M)\n[[B = Y - A]]\n- B = trade balance. Improvement needs **Y to rise more than A** or A to fall\n# Working of the Approach\n- Devaluation raises exports, lowers imports\n- If there are **idle resources,** output Y rises, and B improves if A rises less than Y\n- If there is **full employment,** Y cannot rise, so absorption must be cut\n- Need **expenditure-reducing policy:** tight fiscal and monetary policy\n# Direct Effects on Absorption\n- **Real balance (cash balance) effect:** rise in prices reduces real balances, so spending falls\n- **Money illusion:** people do not adjust fully\n- **Redistribution of income:** to profit earners with a higher saving rate\n- **Terms of trade effect:** worsening of TOT reduces real income\n- **Government revenue effect:** tax revenue rises\n# Conclusions of the Approach\n- Devaluation works if Y rises and A is controlled\n- **Policy mix:** devaluation plus deflation\n# Criticism\n- Ignores the relative price (elasticity) effects\n- Assumes autonomous absorption is independent of devaluation\n- Cannot separate income and absorption effects clearly\n- Does not explain capital flows\n- Marginal propensity to absorb is hard to measure\n# Importance\n- Brought macro analysis into BOP theory\n- Basis for IMF stabilisation programmes\n> Conclusion: The absorption approach explains that devaluation can succeed only with control over domestic spending."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "What do you understand by Agricultural Price Policy? What are its objectives and achievements?", "y": "2018, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Agricultural price policy:** government policy to fix farm prices so that farmers get **remunerative prices** and consumers get **fair prices**\n- **Need:** farm prices are unstable (weather, perishability, inelastic demand)\n- **Started:** 1965-66 with the Green Revolution and food shortage\n- **Agricultural Prices Commission** set up 1965, renamed **CACP** in 1985\n# Institutions\n- **CACP:** recommends MSP to the government\n- **FCI (1965):** procures grain, maintains buffer stock\n- **NAFED:** procures pulses and oilseeds\n- **PDS:** distributes grain at subsidised prices\n# Objectives\n- Give **remunerative price** to farmers, protect from price crash\n- Encourage **adoption of new technology** and higher output\n- Ensure **food security** and **buffer stock**\n- Keep **consumer prices** reasonable\n- Maintain **price parity** between agriculture and industry (terms of trade)\n- Change **cropping pattern** (pulses, oilseeds)\n- Reduce price fluctuation\n# Instruments\n- **Minimum Support Price (MSP):** guaranteed floor price for 22 crops (and FRP for sugarcane)\n- **Procurement price:** price at which government buys, above MSP in some states\n- **Issue price:** price at which PDS sells to consumers\n- **Buffer stock:** stocks to stabilise prices\n- **Statutory minimum price** for sugarcane (now FRP)\n- **Import-export policy** for stabilising price\n# Basis of MSP\n- **A2+FL cost** and **C2 cost** concepts\n- **Swaminathan Commission (2006):** MSP at least **50 percent above C2 cost**\n- **Budget 2018-19:** MSP at least 1.5 times of cost (A2+FL)\n# Achievements\n- **Self-sufficiency** in wheat and rice\n- **Higher production** and farm income security\n- **Buffer stock** and food security\n- **Control over price fluctuation**\n- Spread of HYV technology\n# Shortcomings\n- **Benefit limited to few crops** (wheat, rice) and regions (Punjab, Haryana, MP)\n- **Shanta Kumar Committee (2015):** only about 6 percent of farmers sell at MSP\n- **Pulses and oilseeds** are neglected\n- **Large stocks,** storage cost, wastage\n- **Fiscal burden** of food subsidy\n- **Distorts cropping pattern,** water-guzzling crops\n- **Middlemen and weak procurement** in many states\n- Low awareness among small farmers\n# Reforms and Recent Steps\n- **PM-AASHA (2018):** price support, price deficiency payment\n- **Bhavantar Bhugtan Yojana (MP)**\n- **e-NAM (2016):** national agricultural market\n- **Farm laws 2020** (repealed 2021)\n- Expansion of procurement for pulses and oilseeds\n> Conclusion: Price policy protected farmers and ensured food security, but it needs diversification and better coverage."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit I - Pre-classical", "q": "Critically examine the economic ideas of the Physiocrats.", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Physiocracy:** from Greek physis (nature) and kratos (rule) - rule of nature\n- **Founder:** Francois Quesnay (1694-1774), court physician of Louis XV\n- **Followers:** Mirabeau, Turgot, Dupont de Nemours (coined the word \"physiocratie\"), Mercier de la Riviere, Gournay\n- **Period:** mid-18th century France\n- **Background:** reaction against **Mercantilism** and Colbertism, decline of French agriculture, heavy taxes on farmers\n- **Key works:** Tableau Economique (1758), Maxims of Economic Government, Turgot's Reflections on the Formation and Distribution of Wealth (1766)\n# Main Ideas\n- **1. Natural order:** economic life is governed by natural laws set by God\n- **2. Land is the only source of wealth:** nature (land) alone gives surplus\n- **3. Agriculture alone is productive:** industry and trade only transform wealth\n- **4. Produit net (net product):** output of agriculture minus cost of production; the surplus goes as rent to landlords\n- **5. Three classes of society:**\n- **Productive class:** farmers and agricultural labourers\n- **Proprietary class:** landlords, king, church\n- **Sterile class:** artisans, merchants, manufacturers\n- **6. Circular flow of income:** money and goods move among the three classes\n- **7. Single tax (impot unique):** a single tax on landlords' net product\n- **8. Laissez faire, laissez passer:** freedom of trade, no state interference\n- **9. Private property and individual liberty** as natural rights\n- **10. Free internal trade in grain** and end of guild restrictions\n- **11. High price of agricultural products** to help farmers' capital formation\n- **12. Capital (avances):** original, annual and ground advances\n- **13. Role of the state:** protect life, property and contracts, enforce the natural order\n- **14. Enlightened despotism:** a strong, educated ruler to enforce natural law\n- **15. Against Mercantilism:** wealth is not gold and silver, but land produce\n# Tableau Economique (1758)\n- **A zig-zag diagram** showing circulation of income among classes\n- **Farmers** pay rent to landlords, buy goods from artisans\n- **Landlords** spend on agricultural products and manufactured goods\n- **Artisans** buy raw material and food from farmers\n- **Money returns** to farmers, ready for the next year\n- **Equilibrium** if flow is repeated each year\n- **First macro model** of the economy\n# Criticism\n- **Wrong to call industry and trade sterile**\n- **Land only source of value,** ignoring labour and capital\n- **Single tax is impractical** and heavy on landlords\n- **Based on French conditions,** narrow view\n- **Natural order** is assumed without proof\n- **Ignores role of foreign trade**\n- **Ignores division of labour** (Smith)\n- **Unrealistic distinction** of classes\n- **Political:** enlightened despotism contradicts liberty\n# Contributions\n- **First systematic school** of economics\n- **Idea of surplus,** basis of Ricardo and Marx\n- **Circular flow** and input-output analysis (Leontief)\n- **Laissez-faire** philosophy, free trade\n- **Influence on Adam Smith,** Wealth of Nations (1776)\n- **Single tax:** Henry George later\n- **Marx called Quesnay** the father of modern political economy\n> Conclusion: Physiocrats erred about industry, but they gave economics a scientific start and the idea of a self-regulating economy."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit I - Classical", "q": "Describe Malthus' ideas on population and over-production (general glut).", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Thomas Robert Malthus (1766-1834):** English clergyman, professor at East India College (Haileybury)\n- **Famous works:** **Essay on the Principle of Population (1798)**, 2nd edition 1803, **Principles of Political Economy (1820)**\n- **Other works:** Inquiry into Rent (1815), Observations on Corn Laws (1814)\n- **Context:** population growth, French Revolution, Poor Laws, Godwin's utopia\n# Theory of Population\n- **Population increases in geometric ratio** (1, 2, 4, 8, 16) every 25 years\n- **Food supply increases in arithmetic ratio** (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\n- **Law of diminishing returns** on land supports this view\n- **Population is limited by means of subsistence**\n- **Population tends to press** against food supply\n- **Gap leads to poverty,** vice and misery\n# Checks to Population\n- **Preventive checks (foresight):**\n- **Moral restraint:** late marriage and celibacy (added in 2nd edition)\n- **Vice:** birth control, prostitution (condemned by Malthus)\n- **Positive checks (nature):**\n- **Famine, disease, epidemics, war**\n- **Misery** that raises death rate\n- **Result:** population adjusts to food supply\n# Application of the Theory\n- **Opposed English Poor Laws:** relief raises births and worsens poverty\n- **Opposed Godwin and Condorcet's** utopian ideas\n- **Subsistence theory of wages** was supported\n# Other Contributions\n- **Theory of rent (1815):** rent arises from fertility and scarcity (published with Ricardo and West)\n- **Theory of value:** demand and supply\n- **Support for Corn Laws:** protection to agriculture\n- **Theory of general glut:** possibility of over-production\n- **Against Say's law:** supply does not create its own demand\n- **Effective demand** concept (Keynes praised Malthus)\n- **Under-consumption** explanation of crises\n- **Unproductive consumers:** landlords, church, state servants should spend\n- **Public works** to maintain demand\n- **Controversy with Ricardo** on gluts\n# Criticism of Population Theory\n- **Technology raised food supply** (green revolution)\n- **Birth rate falls** with income and education (demographic transition)\n- **Food imports** and trade ignored\n- **Rates are not fixed** geometric and arithmetic\n- **Ignores family planning** and contraception\n- **Poor Law view** is harsh and unfair to the poor\n- **Ignores social causes** of poverty\n# Importance\n- **Influenced Darwin** and Wallace (struggle for existence)\n- **Influenced Ricardo,** wage fund\n- **Anticipated Keynes** on effective demand\n- **Basis of modern population economics** and the concept of Malthusian trap\n- **Neo-Malthusians:** limits to growth, environment\n> Conclusion: Many predictions failed, but his warnings on resources and his idea of effective demand remain valuable."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit I - Welfare", "q": "Critically analyse the marginalist approach to the theory of the firm.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Marginalist theory of the firm:** firm is a rational unit aiming at **profit maximisation**\n- **Basis:** neo-classical economics, marginal analysis\n- **Decision rule:** equate marginal cost with marginal revenue\n# Assumptions\n- Single product firm, owner-manager (entrepreneur)\n- **Profit maximisation** is the only goal\n- **Perfect knowledge** of demand and cost\n- Rational behaviour and given objectives\n- Short run (fixed plant) and long run\n- Known cost and revenue curves\n# Equilibrium Conditions\n- **First order condition:** MC = MR\n[[MC = MR]]\n- **Second order condition:** MC cuts MR from below\n- Profit = TR minus TC is maximum at this output\n- Under perfect competition **P = MR = MC**\n- Under monopoly **P is above MC**\n# Short Run and Long Run\n- Short run: firm may earn supernormal profit, normal profit or loss, shut down if P < AVC\n- Long run: in perfect competition, P = LAC = LMC\n# Hall and Hitch (1939) Criticism\n- Surveyed **38 firms** in Oxford\n- Firms do not know MC and MR curves\n- They follow **full-cost pricing** (average cost plus mark-up)\n- Prices are stable, not changed by small change in demand\n# Other Criticisms\n- **Baumol:** firms maximise **sales revenue** subject to minimum profit\n- **Marris:** firms maximise **growth rate**\n- **Williamson:** managers maximise **utility** (staff, perquisites)\n- **Simon:** firms are **satisficers,** not maximisers\n- **Cyert and March:** behavioural theory, multiple goals\n- **Berle and Means:** separation of ownership and control\n- Uncertainty and imperfect information\n- Interdependence in oligopoly is ignored\n- Difficulty in measuring MC and MR\n# Defence of Marginalism\n- **Machlup:** firms do not calculate MC and MR but behave as if they do\n- **Friedman:** judge theory by predictions, not assumptions\n- **Competition** forces firms to maximise profits (survival)\n- Gives a simple and powerful basis for analysis\n# Importance\n- Foundation of price theory, supply, factor pricing\n> Conclusion: Marginalism is a good base model, but it needs behavioural and managerial theories to complete it."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Explain price leadership and the dominant firm model of price leadership.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Price leadership:** one firm in an oligopoly sets the price and others follow\n- It is a **tacit (informal) understanding,** no formal agreement\n- **Aim:** to avoid price war and reduce uncertainty\n- **Examples:** steel, cement, cigarettes, oil in the past\n# Types of Price Leadership\n- **Dominant firm (low cost) leadership:** the largest firm leads\n- **Barometric leadership:** a firm that predicts the market well leads (not the largest)\n- **Collusive leadership:** leader is chosen under an agreement\n- **Exploitative leadership:** a strong firm forces others to follow\n# Dominant Firm Model: Assumptions\n- One **large firm** and many **small firms**\n- **Homogeneous** product\n- **Small firms are price takers,** they follow the leader price\n- The large firm knows the market demand and the supply of small firms\n- Large firm has **lower costs**\n# Working of the Model\n- **Market demand curve (DD)** is known\n- **Supply of small firms (sum of their MC curves)** at each price is found\n- **Leader's demand = market demand minus small firms' supply**\n- Leader's demand curve is the **residual demand curve**\n- Leader derives **MR** from this residual demand\n- Leader sets output where **MC = MR**\n[[Leader: MC(L) = MR(L)]]\n- This gives leader's price **P***\n- Small firms take P* and produce where **their MC = P***\n# Result\n- **Total output = leader's output + small firms' output**\n- It equals market demand at P*\n- Leader has a large share, small firms have the rest\n# Merits\n- Explains stable pricing in oligopoly\n- Avoids price war\n# Demerits\n- Leader loses share in the long run (small firms grow)\n- **Entry of new firms** affects stability\n- Assumes small firms always follow\n- Barometric leadership is hard to identify\n- Ignores product differentiation\n# Evaluation\n- Legal restrictions (Competition Act) can affect it\n> Conclusion: Price leadership is a practical way to avoid price war while keeping the market stable."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Discuss the nature of oligopoly. Examine Bertrand model of duopoly and say why it is better than Cournot model.", "y": "2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Oligopoly:** market with a few sellers, each with a significant share\n- **Types:** pure (homogeneous, e.g. cement) and differentiated (e.g. cars)\n- **Duopoly:** special case of two sellers\n# Nature (Features) of Oligopoly\n- **Few sellers,** big in size\n- **Interdependence:** each firm considers rivals' reactions\n- **Barriers to entry:** capital, patents, scale\n- **Price rigidity:** prices are sticky\n- **Non-price competition:** advertising, quality\n- **Indeterminate demand curve:** reactions of rivals unknown\n- **Group behaviour:** collusion or competition\n- **Uncertainty** about rivals' policy\n- **No single theory** of oligopoly\n# Bertrand Model (1883)\n- **Joseph Bertrand** criticised Cournot\n- **Decision variable is price,** not output\n- **Assumptions:** two firms, identical product and same cost, firms take rival's **price** as given, consumers buy from the cheaper seller\n# Working\n- Firm A sets monopoly price\n- B sets slightly lower price and captures the whole market\n- A then undercuts B\n- Price cutting continues till **P = MC**\n[[Equilibrium: P = MC ; profit = 0]]\n- No firm will lower price below MC\n# Result\n- **Price equals the competitive price,** profits are zero\n- Two firms are enough to get a competitive result (Bertrand paradox)\n# Comparison with Cournot\n- **Cournot:** output is the variable, positive profits\n- **Bertrand:** price is the variable, zero profit\n- Cournot: reaction curves slope down. Bertrand: reaction curves slope up\n# Is Bertrand Better?\n- **Yes:** firms in practice announce prices, so price is a realistic variable\n- **No:** ignores capacity limits (Edgeworth), product differentiation\n- With capacity limits, there is no pure strategy equilibrium\n- Result of P = MC is not seen in the real world\n# Criticism\n- Assumes undercutting without limit\n- Ignores collusion and dynamic behaviour\n- Product homogeneity is assumed\n> Conclusion: Bertrand is a useful improvement on Cournot, but both are partial models of oligopoly."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit V - Growth and Cycles", "q": "Explain Harrod growth model: types of growth rates (warranted, actual, natural).", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **R. F. Harrod,** An Essay in Dynamic Theory (1939)\n- **Aim:** to find the growth rate for steady full-capacity growth\n- **Extension of Keynes** to the long run (Keynes: short run)\n- **Domar** gave a similar model, hence the **Harrod-Domar model**\n# Assumptions\n- Closed economy, no government\n- Saving is a **fixed proportion (s) of income**\n- **Capital-output ratio** is constant\n- No depreciation, no technical change\n- Full employment initially\n- Labour and capital are in fixed proportion\n# Basic Equation\n- **Saving = Investment** for equilibrium\n[[G x C = s]]\n- G = rate of growth, C = capital-output ratio, s = propensity to save\n# Three Growth Rates\n- **Actual rate (G):** G = s / Ca (Ca = actual capital-output ratio)\n- **Warranted rate (Gw):** growth rate at which entrepreneurs are satisfied, capacity is fully used\n[[Gw = s / Cr]]\n- **Natural rate (Gn):** maximum growth permitted by **labour force and technical progress**\n# Relationship among the Rates\n- **G = Gw:** steady growth, equilibrium\n- **G > Gw:** actual demand exceeds capacity, investment rises, **cumulative boom**\n- **G < Gw:** excess capacity, investment falls, **cumulative depression**\n- **Gw > Gn:** unemployment and stagnation (secular)\n- **Gw < Gn:** full-employment boom, rising prices\n# Knife-Edge Problem\n- Equilibrium growth is **unstable**\n- Any deviation of G from Gw increases (cumulative)\n- Like balancing on a knife's edge\n- Need **G = Gw = Gn** for full-employment steady growth, which is rare\n# Policy Implications\n- **Raise saving rate** to increase growth\n- Reduce capital-output ratio by technology\n- Adjust Gw and Gn through policy\n- **Government planning** to maintain balance\n# Criticism\n- Fixed capital-output ratio and saving rate are unreal\n- Fixed factor proportions, no substitution (Solow)\n- Closed economy, no state\n- Ignores price changes, financial factors\n- Knife-edge result is a model feature, not reality\n# Importance\n- Basis of the planning model in developing countries (India Second Plan)\n- Starting point for modern growth theory\n> Conclusion: The Harrod model highlights the instability of growth, but its rigid assumptions limit practical use."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit V - Growth and Cycles", "q": "Explain Samuelson theory of trade cycle.", "y": "2017", "a": "# Introduction\n- **P. A. Samuelson (1939):** Interactions Between the Multiplier Analysis and the Principle of Acceleration\n- **Idea:** interaction of multiplier and accelerator causes income cycles\n- **Features:** mathematical model with difference equations\n# Basic Concepts\n- **Multiplier:** change in income from investment, depends on MPC (c)\n- **Accelerator (v):** induced investment depends on change in consumption\n- **Lag:** consumption depends on the previous period's income\n# Model\n[[Yt = Ct + It + Gt]]\n[[Ct = c Y(t-1)]]\n[[It = v (Ct - C(t-1))]]\n- Government spending G is constant\n- Combine into a second-order difference equation\n# Working\n- A rise in G raises income (multiplier)\n- Higher income raises consumption\n- Higher consumption raises induced investment (accelerator)\n- That raises income again\n- When consumption growth slows, investment falls, income falls\n# Four Possible Paths\n- **Damped non-cyclical:** income moves smoothly to a new level\n- **Damped cyclical:** cycles die out gradually\n- **Explosive cyclical:** cycles become larger\n- **Explosive non-cyclical:** income rises or falls steadily\n# Which Path Depends on c and v\n- **Low c, low v:** damped path\n- **High v:** explosive path\n- **Cycles occur** when v is large relative to c\n- **c and v values** give four regions in a diagram\n# Merits\n- Shows cycles can be **internally generated**\n- Combines two important concepts\n- Gives clear numerical analysis\n# Criticism\n- Fixed c and v are unrealistic\n- **No ceiling or floor,** so explosive cycle never ends (Hicks improved this)\n- Ignores money, prices, expectations\n- No actual turning points\n- Accelerator works only with spare capacity\n# Importance\n- Starting point for cycle theories (Hicks, Goodwin)\n> Conclusion: Samuelson's model explains how cycles arise from internal forces, but it is a limited and mechanical explanation."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit IV - Money and IS-LM", "q": "What is general equilibrium? Describe any one model of general equilibrium (Walras).", "y": "2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Leon Walras** (Elements of Pure Economics, 1874)\n- **Partial equilibrium (Marshall):** one market, others constant\n- **General equilibrium:** all markets in equilibrium **simultaneously**\n- **Reason:** markets are interdependent\n# Basic Idea\n- **Demand for a good** depends on the prices of all goods and factors\n- **Supply** depends on all prices\n- Equilibrium is a set of prices at which **all markets clear**\n# Walrasian Model\n- Economy with **households and firms**\n- **Consumers** maximise utility, **firms** maximise profits\n- **Four types of markets:** products, factors, capital, money\n- **Numeraire:** one commodity as the unit for expressing prices\n- Number of equations is equal to number of unknowns\n# Equations\n- n goods, n-1 independent prices\n- **Demand = supply** for each good\n- **Budget constraint** of each household\n- **Zero profit** condition for each firm (in the long run)\n# Walras' Law\n- **Sum of values of excess demands in all markets is zero**\n[[Sum Pi x Ei = 0]]\n- So if n-1 markets are in equilibrium, the nth is also in equilibrium\n- Money market is in the model (the numeraire)\n# Tatonnement Process\n- **Auctioneer** announces a price\n- Excess demand leads to a rise in price, excess supply leads to a fall\n- No trade till equilibrium is reached\n- Process continues till excess demand is zero\n# Existence, Uniqueness, Stability\n- **Existence:** Arrow and Debreu (1954) proved it with fixed-point theorems\n- **Uniqueness:** need conditions on demand (gross substitutes)\n- **Stability:** equilibrium should be reached by adjustment\n# Welfare Aspects\n- **First welfare theorem:** competitive equilibrium is Pareto optimal\n- **Second welfare theorem:** any Pareto optimum can be reached by redistribution\n# Criticism\n- Perfect competition and perfect information are unrealistic\n- Static, no time or uncertainty\n- Auctioneer is imaginary\n- Ignores externalities, public goods, monopoly\n- Money plays a minor role\n- Very high abstraction\n# Importance\n- Foundation of modern theory, welfare economics, international trade and macro microfoundations\n> Conclusion: General equilibrium offers a powerful framework, though based on strong assumptions."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit III - Terms of Trade", "q": "What do you mean by gains from trade? Gains from exchange and gains from specialisation.", "y": "2016, 2018, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Gains from trade:** benefits that a country gets from trade compared to no trade (autarky)\n- **Discussed by:** Smith, Ricardo, Mill, Marshall, Haberler\n# Sources of Gains\n- **Gains from exchange:** consumers get goods at a cost lower than domestic cost\n- **Gains from specialisation:** world output rises as countries produce where comparative advantage lies\n- **Both together** make the total gain\n# Gains from Exchange\n- Terms of trade differ from the domestic price ratio\n- A country exports a good and gets more imports per unit than it can produce at home\n- Production is **fixed** at the pre-trade point, consumption moves to a higher indifference curve\n- Example: India 1 cloth = 1 wheat, world 1 cloth = 2 wheat, so India gains one wheat per cloth\n# Gains from Specialisation\n- Country specialises in the good with the **lower opportunity cost**\n- **PPC production point moves** to the specialisation point\n- Output of the world is larger\n- Gains arise from comparative advantage\n# Ricardian Example\n- Labour hours: England: cloth 100, wine 120. Portugal: cloth 90, wine 80\n- Portugal has absolute advantage in both\n- England has comparative advantage in cloth, Portugal in wine\n- Both gain by specialisation\n# Division of Gains\n- **Depends on terms of trade**\n- TOT closer to the other country's domestic ratio means more gain for you\n- **Mill:** depends on reciprocal demand\n- A small country usually gets a larger share of the gain\n# Measurement of Gains\n- Gains in terms of indifference curves (community)\n- Increase in real income\n# Dynamic Gains\n- **Economies of scale** from a larger market\n- **Transfer of technology** and ideas\n- **Competition** raises efficiency\n- **Investment and growth**\n- **Wider choice,** better quality\n# Limitations (Arguments Against)\n- Distribution of gains unequal\n- **Terms of trade** may worsen for developing countries (Prebisch)\n- Dependence on exports of primary goods\n- Infant industry may suffer\n- Domestic unemployment due to imports\n> Conclusion: Trade raises welfare for both countries, but the division of gains depends on terms of trade."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Discuss the merits and demerits of fixed and flexible exchange rates.", "y": "2016, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Exchange rate:** price of one currency in terms of another\n- **Fixed exchange rate:** rate is fixed by the government or central bank (gold standard, Bretton Woods 1944-71)\n- **Flexible exchange rate:** rate is determined by **demand and supply** in the forex market\n- **Intermediate systems:** managed float, crawling peg, currency board\n# Merits of Fixed Exchange Rate\n- **Stability and certainty,** helps trade and investment\n- **Encourages long-term capital flows**\n- **Checks speculation** and instability\n- **Anti-inflationary discipline** on government\n- **Easy planning** for trade and prices\n- **International cooperation** (IMF system)\n# Demerits of Fixed Exchange Rate\n- **Needs large reserves** for defence of the rate\n- **Loss of independent monetary policy** (trilemma)\n- **Rigid:** disequilibrium persists, rate becomes overvalued or undervalued\n- **Black market** and exchange control\n- **Sudden devaluation** causes shock\n- **Imports inflation or deflation** from partner countries\n- Speculative attacks on currency (1992 EMS crisis)\n# Merits of Flexible Exchange Rate\n- **Automatic BOP adjustment**\n- **No need for large reserves**\n- **Policy independence** in monetary and fiscal policy\n- **Shock absorber:** currency changes absorb external shocks\n- **No exchange control,** free trade\n- **Simple, market-driven**\n# Demerits of Flexible Exchange Rate\n- **Uncertainty** for traders and investors\n- **Speculation and volatility**\n- **May cause inflation** (depreciation raises import cost)\n- **Hampers foreign investment**\n- **Destabilising** for small developing countries\n- **Competitive depreciation** possible\n# Mundell's Trilemma\n- A country cannot have fixed exchange rate, free capital flow and independent monetary policy together\n# Middle Path\n- **Managed float:** market determines, central bank intervenes\n- **Crawling peg:** small regular changes\n- India follows managed float since 1993\n> Conclusion: Neither system is perfect; most countries today use managed flexibility."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Explain devaluation, its objectives and the Marshall-Lerner condition.", "y": "2018, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Devaluation:** official lowering of the value of the currency in terms of foreign currency (fixed exchange rate system)\n- **Depreciation:** fall due to market forces (flexible rate)\n- **Revaluation:** opposite of devaluation\n# Objectives\n- **Correct BOP deficit**\n- **Increase exports** and discourage imports\n- **Promote employment** and domestic industry\n- **Increase foreign exchange reserves**\n- **Remove overvaluation** of currency\n- **Reduce trade deficit**\n# Working\n- Exports become cheaper in foreign currency, so foreign demand rises\n- Imports become costlier, so import demand falls\n- Trade balance improves if demand is elastic\n# Marshall-Lerner Condition\n[[Ex + Em > 1]]\n- Devaluation improves BOP if **sum of elasticities of demand for exports and imports is greater than one**\n- If sum is equal to one, no change. If less than one, BOP worsens\n# Robinson-Metzler (Bickerdike) Condition\n- Includes supply elasticities also\n# J-Curve Effect\n- **Short run:** trade deficit worsens (contracts fixed, quantities do not adjust)\n- **Long run:** deficit falls as quantities adjust\n- The curve looks like the letter J\n# Favourable Conditions\n- Elastic demand and supply\n- Spare capacity\n- Others do not retaliate\n- Domestic prices do not rise\n# Limitations\n- **Inflation** from costlier imports\n- **Retaliation** (competitive devaluation)\n- Low elasticities in developing countries\n- **Debt burden** rises (foreign loans)\n- Supply constraints\n- Uncertainty and loss of confidence\n# Absorption View\n- Devaluation works only if domestic absorption is cut\n# India's Experience\n- **1949:** after sterling devaluation\n- **1966:** devaluation by about 57 percent, unsuccessful\n- **1991:** two-step downward adjustment of about 18-19 percent as part of reforms\n> Conclusion: Devaluation can correct BOP deficit only under favourable elasticity and supporting policies."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "Discuss functions, objectives and achievements of IMF. How has India benefitted?", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **IMF:** International Monetary Fund\n- **Established:** Bretton Woods Conference, 1944 (came into being 1945, began operations 1947)\n- **Headquarters:** Washington DC\n- **Members:** about 190 countries\n- **Sister organisation:** World Bank\n# Objectives (Article I)\n- **Promote international monetary cooperation**\n- **Facilitate expansion of world trade** and balanced growth\n- **Promote exchange rate stability** and orderly arrangements\n- **Establish a multilateral payments system,** remove exchange restrictions\n- **Give temporary financial help** to countries in BOP difficulty\n- **Shorten and reduce** the degree of BOP disequilibrium\n# Functions\n- **Financial function:** lending to members through quotas, facilities\n- **Regulatory function:** code of conduct on exchange rate policies\n- **Surveillance:** Article IV consultation, monitoring of economies\n- **Technical assistance** and training\n- **Creation of reserve asset:** SDR (1969)\n- **Research and data:** World Economic Outlook, statistics\n# Resources\n- **Quota subscriptions** of members (basis of voting, borrowing)\n- **Borrowing** (GAB, NAB)\n- **SDR allocation**\n- **Gold sales**\n# Lending Facilities\n- **Stand-By Arrangement (SBA)**\n- **Extended Fund Facility (EFF)**\n- **Flexible Credit Line**\n- **Rapid Financing Instrument**\n- **Poverty Reduction and Growth Trust** (concessional)\n# Achievements\n- Helped many countries in BOP crises\n- Stability in exchange rates for a long time (till 1971)\n- Created SDR as an international reserve asset\n- Helped in the debt crises of the 1980s, Asian crisis and 2008-09 crisis\n- Supported the transition economies\n- Standard of economic data and research\n# Criticism\n- **Conditionality** is harsh (austerity), hurts the poor\n- **Dominated by rich countries** (voting by quota)\n- Failed in the East Asian crisis (1997)\n- One-size-fits-all policy\n- Failed to prevent the breakdown of Bretton Woods\n# India and the IMF\n- Founder member\n- Borrowed in 1981 (EFF, about SDR 5 billion) and in 1991 (BOP crisis)\n- No borrowing in recent decades\n- India seeks greater quota and voice\n> Conclusion: The IMF plays a vital role in global stability, but needs governance reform and flexible conditions."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "Distinguish between Foreign Direct Investment and Portfolio Investment.", "y": "2018, 2022", "a": "# Meaning\n- **FDI:** investment by a foreign entity in a business with **lasting interest and control** (generally 10 percent or more equity)\n- **FPI:** investment in **shares, bonds and securities** for returns, **without control** (less than 10 percent)\n# Differences\n- **Control:** FDI gives management control, FPI does not\n- **Time horizon:** FDI is long-term, FPI is short-term\n- **Nature of capital:** FDI is physical (plants, machines) plus technology; FPI is financial\n- **Stability:** FDI is stable; FPI is volatile (hot money)\n- **Risk:** FDI risk is borne by the investor in the business; FPI can exit quickly\n- **Impact on growth:** FDI raises output, employment and technology; FPI affects stock markets and liquidity\n- **Exit:** FDI is difficult to withdraw; FPI easy to sell\n- **Return:** FDI gets profit and dividends; FPI gets capital gains and dividends\n- **Technology transfer:** FDI yes; FPI no\n- **Regulation in India:** FDI by FEMA and FDI policy (automatic route and approval route); FPI by SEBI (FPI Regulations)\n- **Role of host government:** FDI sector caps; FPI investment limits (aggregate)\n# Merits of FDI\n- Capital inflow and technology\n- Employment, exports and competition\n- Management skills and global linkages\n- Stable source of foreign exchange\n# Demerits of FDI\n- Profit repatriation, outflow of dividends\n- Dominance of multinational companies, harms domestic industry\n- Environmental and cultural concerns\n- Political influence\n# Merits of FPI\n- Liquidity in markets, lower cost of capital\n- Deepening of capital markets\n- Quick supply of foreign capital\n# Demerits of FPI\n- **Sudden outflow** can cause a currency or stock market crash (1997 Asian crisis, 2013 taper tantrum)\n- Exchange rate volatility\n- Speculation and no real addition to capacity\n# India\n- FDI in sectors through automatic route, caps in some (defence, insurance)\n- FPI replaced FII (2014 SEBI norms)\n> Conclusion: FDI is preferred for development, FPI is useful but needs careful monitoring."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Discuss opportunity cost theory of Haberler.", "y": "2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Gottfried Haberler** (The Theory of International Trade, 1933)\n- **Background:** Ricardo's comparative cost theory used the **labour theory of value,** which is defective\n- **Haberler's contribution:** restated comparative advantage in terms of **opportunity cost**\n- **Opportunity cost:** quantity of a good given up to produce one more unit of another\n# Assumptions\n- Two countries, two goods, two factors\n- Perfect competition, full employment\n- Free trade, no transport cost\n- Factors are mobile within, immobile between countries\n- Production possibility curve (PPC) is given\n- Technology is given\n# Production Possibility Curve\n- **PPC shows** the maximum combination of two goods with given resources\n- **Slope of PPC** at any point = marginal rate of transformation (MRT)\n- **MRT = opportunity cost** of one good in terms of another\n# Cases\n- **Constant opportunity cost:** straight line PPC (Ricardo's case)\n- **Increasing opportunity cost:** concave PPC (realistic)\n- **Decreasing cost:** convex (unusual)\n# Comparative Advantage\n- **A country has comparative advantage** in the good whose opportunity cost is lower\n- It exports that good and imports the other\n- Example: India: 1 cloth = 2 wheat. Britain: 1 cloth = 1 wheat. India has an advantage in wheat, Britain in cloth\n# Equilibrium with Trade\n- **Price ratio** (terms of trade) is between the two domestic opportunity cost ratios\n- Production is where **MRT = price ratio**\n- Consumption is where **indifference curve is tangent to price line**\n- Country gains by moving to a higher indifference curve\n# Gains from Trade\n- Exchange gains and specialisation gains\n- Both countries gain\n# Merits\n- Free from labour theory of value\n- Includes all factors of production\n- Realistic: increasing costs\n- Uses PPC and indifference curve, more general\n- Explains complete and partial specialisation\n# Criticism\n- Does not explain why opportunity costs differ (needs Heckscher-Ohlin)\n- Static and ignores economies of scale\n- Assumes perfect competition\n- Ignores transport and tariffs\n- Based on a two-country model\n> Conclusion: Haberler generalised Ricardo's theory by using opportunity cost, giving a stronger theoretical base."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "What are Regional Trade Agreements? Discuss their effects.", "y": "2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **RTA:** agreement among a group of countries to reduce or remove barriers to trade within the group\n- **Regionalism vs multilateralism:** WTO allows RTAs under **GATT Article XXIV**\n- **Examples:** EU, USMCA (formerly NAFTA), ASEAN, SAFTA, Mercosur\n# Stages (Forms) of Integration\n- **Preferential Trade Area:** lower tariffs among members (India-Afghanistan)\n- **Free Trade Area:** no tariffs inside, each keeps its own external tariff (SAFTA, NAFTA)\n- **Customs Union:** free trade inside plus **common external tariff** (Mercosur)\n- **Common Market:** free movement of labour and capital also (EEC earlier)\n- **Economic Union:** common economic policy (EU)\n- **Monetary Union:** common currency (Eurozone)\n# Static Effects (Viner)\n- **Trade creation:** shift from high-cost domestic producer to low-cost partner, welfare rises\n- **Trade diversion:** shift from low-cost outsider to high-cost partner, welfare falls\n- **Net effect** depends on the relative size\n# Dynamic Effects\n- **Economies of scale** in a larger market\n- **More competition,** efficiency\n- **Investment** inflow, FDI\n- **Technology transfer**\n- **Growth** and employment\n# Merits\n- Expands the market and trade\n- Political and strategic cooperation\n- Faster tariff reduction than WTO\n- Attracts FDI\n# Demerits\n- **Discrimination** against non-members\n- **Trade diversion** and inefficiency\n- Weakens **multilateral system** (spaghetti bowl effect: overlapping rules)\n- Loss of sovereignty\n- Unequal gains for small members\n- Revenue loss\n# India's RTAs\n- SAFTA, India-ASEAN FTA\n- CEPA with Japan, South Korea, UAE (2022)\n- India-Australia ECTA (2022)\n- India stayed out of **RCEP** (2019)\n# Evaluation\n- RTAs are a second-best option; their gain depends on trade creation\n> Conclusion: RTAs can raise welfare if trade creation dominates, but they can also harm global free trade."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "Discuss the National Agriculture Policy and causes of low agricultural growth.", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Agriculture:** employs about 45 percent of workers, contributes about 15-18 percent of GDP\n- **National Agriculture Policy, 2000:** first comprehensive policy\n- **Target:** growth rate of **more than 4 percent per annum**\n- **National Policy for Farmers 2007:** focus on farmers' welfare (after Swaminathan Commission)\n# Objectives of the Policy 2000\n- Achieve **over 4 percent annual growth**\n- **Growth based on efficient use of resources,** conserve soil, water, biodiversity\n- **Equitable growth** across regions and farmers\n- **Demand-driven** and technology-oriented agriculture\n- **Sustainable** development\n- Compete in **global markets** (WTO)\n# Main Features\n- **Rural infrastructure:** roads, markets, storage, power\n- **Land and water use,** soil conservation, watershed\n- **Credit:** KCC, institutional credit\n- **Risk management:** crop insurance\n- **Agro-processing** and value addition\n- **Price protection:** MSP, market intervention\n- **Research and extension:** ICAR, KVKs\n- **Cooperatives and Panchayats** role\n- **Export promotion** of agricultural products\n- **Bio-technology** and organic farming\n- **Participation of women and tribal farmers**\n# Causes of Low Agricultural Growth\n- **Monsoon dependence;** only about half of net sown area is irrigated\n- **Small and fragmented holdings** (average size about 1.1 hectare)\n- **Low productivity** compared to world standards\n- **Low public investment** in irrigation, roads, storage\n- **Inadequate credit,** moneylender dependence\n- **Weak marketing,** middlemen, price fluctuation\n- **Incomplete land reforms,** tenancy\n- **Use of old technology,** low seed replacement\n- **Soil degradation,** overuse of fertilisers, water table fall\n- **Climate change** risks\n- **Poor extension services**\n# Measures Taken\n- **PMKSY** (irrigation), **PMFBY** (insurance, 2016)\n- **Soil Health Card (2015)**\n- **e-NAM,** FPOs\n- **PM-KISAN (2019):** Rs 6,000 per year income support\n- **Agriculture Infrastructure Fund (2020)**\n- **Doubling Farmers' Income** target by 2022 (Dalwai Committee)\n- **KCC,** MSP hike, micro irrigation\n# Suggestions\n- Increase investment, irrigation, market reforms\n- Diversification to horticulture, livestock, fisheries\n- Technology, extension and farmer training\n- Land consolidation, FPOs and contract farming\n> Conclusion: Growth in agriculture needs investment, technology and market reform together."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit II - Industry", "q": "Discuss the major components of Industrial Policy development (1956, 1991).", "y": "2016, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Industrial policy:** government's rules and strategy for industrial growth\n- **Aim:** build industrial base, self-reliance, employment, balanced regional growth\n# Industrial Policy Resolution 1948\n- **First industrial policy,** after independence\n- **Mixed economy** accepted\n- **Four categories:** state monopoly (arms, atomic energy, railways); mixed (coal, iron and steel, shipbuilding, mineral oils); government-regulated private sector (18 key industries); and the rest left to private sector\n- Importance to **cottage and small industries**\n- **Foreign capital** allowed with regulation\n# Industrial Policy Resolution 1956 (Economic Constitution)\n- **Socialist pattern of society** (Avadi 1955)\n- **Schedule A:** 17 industries, exclusive state responsibility\n- **Schedule B:** 12 industries, progressively state-owned, private supplements\n- **Schedule C:** rest, private sector\n- Emphasis on **heavy industry** (Mahalanobis model)\n- **Small scale and cottage industry** protection\n- **Reduction of regional inequalities**\n- Prevention of monopoly\n# Laws of the Control Period\n- **Industries (Development and Regulation) Act 1951:** licensing\n- **MRTP Act 1969:** control on monopolies\n- **FERA 1973:** control on foreign exchange\n# Policies of 1970s and 1980s\n- **1973:** big houses allowed in core and heavy industry, joint sector\n- **1977 (Janata):** emphasis on small and tiny sector, **District Industries Centres**\n- **1980:** promotion of competition, modernisation\n- **1985-88:** partial liberalisation, **MRTP limit raised to Rs 100 crore, broad-banding, MODVAT**\n# New Industrial Policy 1991\n- **Abolition of industrial licensing,** except for 18 industries\n- **Public sector reserved industries cut** to 8 (now very few)\n- **MRTP limits removed,** only unfair trade practice\n- **FDI:** automatic approval up to 51 percent in 34 high priority industries\n- **Foreign technology agreements:** automatic permission\n- **Disinvestment** of PSUs\n- **Sick units:** BIFR, policy on revival\n- **Small sector** incentives\n# Later Developments\n- **Make in India (2014),** Start-up India (2016)\n- **MSME Development Act 2006**\n- **National Manufacturing Policy 2011:** 25 percent share of manufacturing in GDP\n- **PLI schemes (2020)**\n- **Atmanirbhar Bharat,** corporate tax cut\n- **GST** (2017), IBC (2016)\n# Evaluation\n- **Merits:** competition, growth, exports, quality, investment, IT boom\n- **Demerits:** jobless growth, weak MSMEs, regional imbalance, share of manufacturing stagnant at about 15 percent\n> Conclusion: Policy moved from control to liberalisation, but manufacturing and employment still need push."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit III - Poverty and Population", "q": "Define poverty. Explain the strategy adopted by India to remove poverty.", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Poverty:** inability to meet minimum basic needs (food, clothing, shelter, health, education)\n- **Absolute poverty:** below a **poverty line** (minimum consumption)\n- **Relative poverty:** compared with other groups\n- **Multidimensional poverty:** deprivation in health, education, living standard\n# Measurement in India\n- **Poverty line:** based on calorie norm (2400 rural, 2100 urban)\n- **Head Count Ratio (HCR):** share of population below the line\n- **Alagh (1979), Lakdawala (1993), Tendulkar (2009), Rangarajan (2014)** committees\n- **Tendulkar:** 21.9 percent in 2011-12 (Rangarajan estimate 29.5 percent)\n- **NITI Aayog MPI:** about 15 percent in 2019-21 (down from about 25 percent in 2015-16)\n# Causes of Poverty\n- **Low growth** in the past, population pressure\n- **Unemployment and underemployment**\n- **Inequality** in income and assets\n- **Illiteracy,** poor health\n- **Low agricultural productivity,** landlessness\n- **Inflation,** regional imbalance\n- **Social factors:** caste, gender\n- **Weak implementation** of schemes\n# Strategy to Remove Poverty\n- **Growth strategy:** higher GDP growth, agriculture, industry, services\n- **Land reforms:** abolition of zamindari, tenancy, ceiling\n- **Self-employment:** IRDP (1978), SGSY (1999), **NRLM (2011)**, PM Mudra\n- **Wage employment:** NREP, JRY, **MGNREGA 2005:** 100 days of work\n- **Food security:** **PDS, NFSA 2013,** mid-day meal\n- **Social security:** NSAP (old-age pension), PM Jeevan Jyoti\n- **Housing:** Indira Awas, **PMAY**\n- **Basic needs:** Jal Jeevan Mission, Ujjwala (2016), Saubhagya\n- **Financial inclusion:** **Jan Dhan Yojana (2014),** DBT\n- **Education and health:** Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan, National Health Mission, Ayushman Bharat\n- **Skill development:** Skill India, DDU-GKY\n- **Urban poverty:** SJSRY, DAY-NULM\n- **Empowerment:** SHGs, Panchayati Raj\n# Evaluation\n- Poverty fell sharply since 1991, but regional gap persists (Bihar, UP, Jharkhand)\n- **Leakages** and weak targeting\n- Overlap of schemes\n- Need for quality jobs\n> Conclusion: Growth with employment, human development and effective delivery is the lasting solution."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit III - Poverty and Population", "q": "Write a note on development of population policy in India.", "y": "2016, 2017", "a": "# Introduction\n- **India's population:** over 140 crore, most populous country (2023)\n- **First country** to launch a family planning programme (1952)\n- **Aim of policy:** reduce fertility, improve health and stabilise population\n# Stages of Development\n- **1952:** National Family Planning Programme launched\n- **1950s-60s:** clinic approach, then extension approach, Lippes loop\n- **1966:** Ministry of Health and Family Planning, targets\n- **1976: first National Population Policy** (Emergency)\n- **1977:** renamed **Family Welfare Programme** (voluntary approach)\n- **1983:** National Health Policy, replacement level goals\n- **1993-94:** Expert group (Swaminathan Committee) for new policy\n- **1996:** target-free approach, **Reproductive and Child Health (RCH)**\n- **2000: National Population Policy**\n- **2005:** National Rural Health Mission\n- **2017:** National Health Policy\n# NPP 1976\n- **Raised age of marriage:** 18 for girls, 21 for boys\n- **Incentives** and disincentives, freeze on Lok Sabha seats\n- **Sterilisation** drive, which became coercive\n- **Backlash** after Emergency\n# NPP 2000: Objectives\n- **Immediate:** meet unmet needs for contraception, health infrastructure and personnel\n- **Medium term:** bring TFR to **replacement level (2.1) by 2010**\n- **Long term:** **stabilise population by 2045**\n# NPP 2000: Targets\n- **IMR** below 30 per 1000\n- **MMR** below 100 per lakh\n- **Universal immunisation**\n- **100 percent registration** of births, deaths, marriages, pregnancy\n- **Delay marriage** to 20 for girls\n- **Universal access** to information and counselling\n- **Promote small family norm**\n- **Prevent and control** AIDS\n- **12 strategic themes**\n# Recent Steps\n- **Mission Parivar Vikas** (2016), injectable contraceptive (Antara)\n- **Two-child norm** in some state panchayats\n- **Janani Suraksha Yojana**\n# Evaluation\n- **TFR is about 2.0** (NFHS-5, 2019-21), near replacement\n- **Regional variation:** Kerala, Tamil Nadu low; Bihar, UP high\n- **Population momentum** keeps numbers rising\n- **Sex ratio** imbalance\n- Weak health services in some states\n> Conclusion: Voluntary approach combined with education, health and women's empowerment gives the best result."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit IV - Environment and Energy", "q": "Outline National Environment Policy of India.", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **National Environment Policy, 2006 (NEP):** comprehensive policy of the Ministry of Environment and Forests\n- **Aim:** **sustainable development,** balance between growth and ecology\n- **Earlier:** National Forest Policy 1988, National Conservation Strategy 1992\n# Objectives\n- **Conservation of critical environmental resources**\n- **Intra-generational equity:** livelihood for the poor\n- **Inter-generational equity**\n- **Integration** of environment into development policy\n- **Efficient use** of resources\n- **Good environmental governance**\n- **Enhance resources** for conservation\n# Guiding Principles\n- **Polluter pays principle**\n- **Precautionary principle**\n- **Public trust doctrine**\n- **Economic efficiency**\n- **Entities with comparative advantage**\n- **Equity**\n- **Legality** and decentralisation\n- **Integration**\n# Legal and Institutional Framework\n- **Wildlife (Protection) Act 1972**\n- **Water Act 1974,** Cess Act 1977\n- **Forest (Conservation) Act 1980**\n- **Air Act 1981**\n- **Environment (Protection) Act 1986**\n- **Biodiversity Act 2002**\n- **Forest Rights Act 2006**\n- **National Green Tribunal Act 2010**\n- **EIA notification 2006**\n- **CPCB and SPCBs**\n- **Article 48A and 51A(g):** state and citizens' duty\n# Climate Policy\n- **NAPCC 2008** with eight missions: Solar, Enhanced Energy Efficiency, Sustainable Habitat, Water, Himalayan Ecosystem, Green India, Sustainable Agriculture, Strategic Knowledge\n- **Paris Agreement 2015,** India's NDCs\n- **COP26 (2021):** 500 GW non-fossil capacity by 2030, **net zero by 2070**\n- **International Solar Alliance (2015)**\n# Key Issue Areas\n- **Land degradation,** forests and wildlife\n- **Biodiversity,** wetlands, coastal areas\n- **Freshwater,** air and noise pollution\n- **Waste management,** hazardous waste\n- **Mining,** urbanisation\n# Programmes\n- **Swachh Bharat Mission**\n- **Namami Gange**\n- **National Clean Air Programme (2019)**\n- **Project Tiger, Project Elephant**\n- **Compensatory Afforestation (CAMPA)**\n# Problems\n- Weak enforcement, pollution of rivers and cities\n- Conflict between development and ecology\n- Deforestation, plastic waste, climate risks\n- Poor coordination among agencies\n> Conclusion: India has a strong framework, but its enforcement and community participation need improvement."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit IV - Environment and Energy", "q": "Why is energy policy essential? Outline India energy policy.", "y": "2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Energy:** basic input for growth, industry, agriculture, transport and households\n- **India:** world's third-largest energy consumer\n- **Energy policy:** ensure secure, affordable and clean energy\n# Why Energy Policy is Essential\n- **Energy security:** India imports about 85 percent of its crude oil\n- **Growth needs:** high demand with rising GDP\n- **Universal access:** electricity and clean cooking for all\n- **Climate commitments:** reduce emissions\n- **Price stability** and subsidy management\n- **Efficient use** of limited resources\n- **Technology** and investment planning\n- **Environment protection**\n- **Reduce import bill** and trade deficit\n# Integrated Energy Policy 2006 (Kirit Parikh Committee)\n- Meet the energy needs of all sectors at **lowest cost**\n- **Energy security,** efficiency, sustainability\n- **Competitive markets,** pricing reforms\n- Targeted subsidy\n- Promotion of **energy efficiency** and R and D\n# Elements of the Policy\n- **Coal:** largest source (about half of commercial energy), clean coal, gasification\n- **Oil and gas:** NELP and HELP (exploration), **strategic petroleum reserves,** city gas, LNG imports\n- **Electricity:** **Electricity Act 2003,** open access, **UDAY (2015),** Saubhagya (2017)\n- **Renewable energy:** **National Solar Mission (2010),** wind, hydro, biomass\n- **Target:** **500 GW non-fossil capacity by 2030**\n- **Nuclear energy:** three-stage programme, Indo-US civil nuclear deal (2008)\n- **Energy efficiency:** Bureau of Energy Efficiency, **star rating, UJALA (LED)**\n- **Biofuels:** ethanol blending (target 20 percent), National Policy on Biofuels\n- **Green hydrogen:** National Green Hydrogen Mission\n- **PM-KUSUM:** solar pumps\n- **International Solar Alliance** (2015)\n- **Electric vehicles:** FAME scheme\n# Problems\n- **Dependence on coal and imported oil**\n- **Losses of electricity distribution companies (discoms)**\n- **Land and finance** for renewables\n- **Grid integration** of renewable sources\n- **Subsidies** and pricing distortions\n- **Pollution** from fossil fuels\n# Suggestions\n- Increase domestic exploration, renewables and storage\n- Reform discoms, smart grids\n- Efficiency and demand management\n- Diversify energy imports\n> Conclusion: India needs a balanced energy mix, with more renewable energy for secure and clean growth."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit V - External Sector", "q": "Discuss main features of India foreign trade policy.", "y": "2018, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Foreign Trade Policy (FTP):** policy on imports and exports of goods and services\n- **Authority:** **DGFT,** Ministry of Commerce, under the **Foreign Trade (Development and Regulation) Act 1992**\n- **Earlier:** Export-Import Policy (1992-97 onwards), renamed FTP in 2009\n- **FTP 2015-20:** MEIS, SEIS\n- **FTP 2023:** effective **1 April 2023**, no fixed end date (dynamic)\n# Objectives\n- **Increase exports** (target **2 trillion dollars by 2030**)\n- **Trade facilitation** and ease of doing business\n- **Self-reliance (Atmanirbhar Bharat)**\n- **Diversify products and markets**\n- **Boost employment**\n# Main Features of FTP 2023\n- **Four pillars:** incentive to remission, export promotion through collaboration, ease of doing business, emerging areas\n- **RoDTEP and RoSCTL:** remission of duties and taxes on exports\n- **Towns of Export Excellence:** four new towns added (Faridabad, Mirzapur, Moradabad, Varanasi)\n- **Districts as Export Hubs** initiative\n- **Merchanting trade** allowed\n- **Amnesty scheme** for pending authorisations\n- **Common digital platform** for certificate of origin\n- **Recognised exporter status** (Star Export Houses)\n- **E-commerce exports** promotion\n- **SCOMET policy** streamlined\n- **Dispute resolution:** one-time settlement\n# Export Promotion Schemes\n- **EPCG:** import of capital goods at zero or low duty with export obligation\n- **Advance Authorisation:** duty-free import of inputs for exports\n- **Duty Drawback**\n- **SEZ (2005), EOU, EPZ**\n- **Market Access Initiative,** TIES\n- **Interest equalisation**\n# Import Policy\n- **Most items freely importable;** few restricted, prohibited (negative list)\n- **Import licensing** for specific goods\n- **Tariff and non-tariff measures,** anti-dumping duty\n# Services\n- **IT and ITeS:** SEIS earlier, STPI, services from India\n# Evaluation\n- **Exports rose** (merchandise and services exceeded about 750 billion dollars in 2022-23)\n- **Persistent trade deficit** (oil, gold, electronics imports)\n- **Concentration** in few products and markets\n- **Non-tariff barriers abroad**\n- **WTO compliance issues** (export subsidies)\n# Suggestions\n- Quality, logistics, scale and technology, FTAs\n> Conclusion: FTP supports exports and ease of trade, but competitiveness is the key to sustained success."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit V - External Sector", "q": "Capital account convertibility: arguments for and against.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Capital account convertibility (CAC):** freedom to convert local currency into foreign currency for **capital transactions** (investment, loans)\n- **Current account convertibility:** for trade and services (India since 1994, Article VIII IMF)\n- **India's position:** **partial** CAC\n# Tarapore Committee (1997)\n- **Chairman:** S. S. Tarapore (RBI)\n- **Three-phase roadmap** (1997-2000)\n- **Pre-conditions:**\n- **Fiscal deficit** to GDP at 3.5 percent\n- **Inflation** at 3-5 percent\n- **CRR** at 3 percent\n- **Gross NPAs** at 5 percent\n- **Monitoring exchange rate band** and adequate reserves\n# Second Tarapore Committee (2006)\n- Five-year phased roadmap, liberalise limits on **ECBs, outward investment, portfolio flows**\n# Arguments in Favour\n- **Free flow of capital** and lower cost of capital\n- **More investment** and growth\n- **Diversification** of portfolio and risk\n- **Integration** with world markets\n- **Discipline** on government policies\n- **Efficient allocation** of resources\n- **Attracts NRI funds**\n# Arguments Against\n- **Volatility and sudden outflow (hot money):** East Asian crisis 1997\n- **Exchange rate instability**\n- **Loss of monetary policy independence** (Mundell trilemma)\n- **Weak financial system** of developing countries\n- **Capital flight** and black money\n- **Asset bubbles,** inflation\n- **Risk for small economies**\n# India's Experience\n- **India escaped** the 1997 Asian crisis (partly due to CAC controls)\n- **FEMA 1999** replaced FERA\n- **FDI** liberalised, **FPI** with limits\n- **ECBs** under ceilings, **LRS** (Liberalised Remittance Scheme) for individuals\n# Present Status\n- Capital account is **partially convertible,** with controls on short-term flows\n- Gradual approach continues\n# Suggestions\n- Strengthen banks, reduce fiscal deficit, build reserves\n- Move step by step, keep safeguards\n> Conclusion: CAC brings benefits only if macro economy and financial institutions are strong enough."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "What is food security? Outline India food security policy.", "y": "2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Food security (FAO):** all people at all times have **physical, social and economic access** to sufficient, safe and nutritious food\n- **Four dimensions:** availability, access, utilisation, stability\n- **Importance:** poverty, health, social stability\n# Components\n- **Availability:** production, stocks, imports\n- **Access:** income, prices, PDS\n- **Absorption:** health, sanitation, nutrition\n- **Stability:** no seasonal or climatic shocks\n# Policy Instruments in India\n- **Production policy:** green revolution, irrigation, MSP\n- **Buffer stock:** held by **FCI** (1965), storage by CWC\n- **Procurement** at MSP\n- **Public Distribution System (PDS)** through fair price shops\n- **Targeted PDS (1997)**\n- **Antyodaya Anna Yojana (2000)**\n- **Mid-day meal** (now **PM POSHAN**), **ICDS** (Anganwadi)\n- **Employment programmes:** MGNREGA\n# National Food Security Act 2013\n- **Legal right** to food\n- **Coverage:** up to **75 percent rural and 50 percent urban population**\n- **Priority households:** **5 kg per person per month** at Rs 3 (rice), Rs 2 (wheat), Re 1 (coarse grains)\n- **Antyodaya households:** **35 kg per household per month**\n- **Maternity benefit:** Rs 6,000 for pregnant women\n- **Nutrition** for children aged 6 months to 14 years\n- **Eldest woman** as head of household for ration card\n- **Grievance redressal** mechanism, state food commissions\n# PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana\n- **Free grain** (5 kg per person) to NFSA beneficiaries, extended for five years from 2024\n# Problems\n- **Leakage and diversion** in PDS\n- **Focus on rice and wheat,** poor nutrition (protein, micronutrients)\n- **Storage losses,** poor godowns\n- **Regional imbalance** in procurement\n- **Exclusion and inclusion errors**\n- **Climate risk,** price rise\n- Hunger and malnutrition remain (Global Hunger Index rank)\n# Reforms\n- **Digitised ration cards,** Aadhaar linking\n- **One Nation One Ration Card**\n- **DBT** and food coupons\n- **Fortified rice**\n- **Diversify** to pulses and millets\n- **Modern storage,** end-to-end computerisation\n> Conclusion: Food security needs production, storage, distribution and nutrition to work together."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit II - Classical and Socialist", "q": "Describe in brief the economic ideas of Karl Marx.", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Karl Marx (1818-83):** German philosopher, economist, revolutionary\n- **Collaborator:** Friedrich Engels\n- **Works:** Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts (1844), Communist Manifesto (1848), Critique of Political Economy (1859), **Das Kapital Vol. 1 (1867)**, Vols 2 and 3 (published by Engels, 1885, 1894)\n- **Influences:** Hegel (dialectics), Feuerbach (materialism), classical economists, French socialists\n# Dialectical and Historical Materialism\n- **Dialectics:** thesis, antithesis, synthesis (change through contradiction)\n- **Materialism:** matter and economic conditions come first\n- **Base and superstructure:** mode of production decides law, politics, religion\n- **Stages of history:** primitive communism, slavery, feudalism, capitalism, socialism, communism\n- **Class struggle:** history is a history of class struggle\n# Labour Theory of Value\n- **Value** is determined by **socially necessary labour time**\n- **Use value** and **exchange value**\n- **Commodity fetishism:** social relations appear as relations between things\n# Surplus Value\n- **Labour power** is a commodity, bought at subsistence wage\n- **Surplus value:** value produced by labour beyond wages, taken by capitalist\n- **Capital:** constant (C, machines) and variable (V, labour)\n- **Rate of surplus value (exploitation) = S/V**\n- **Organic composition of capital = C/V**\n- **Absolute surplus value:** longer working day\n- **Relative surplus value:** higher productivity\n# Capital Accumulation and Capitalist Process\n- **M-C-M':** money to commodity to more money\n- **Accumulation** by reinvestment of surplus\n- **Concentration and centralisation** of capital (big fish eat small)\n- **Industrial reserve army:** unemployed workers keep wages low\n- **Increasing misery** of the proletariat\n# Falling Rate of Profit\n- **Rate of profit = S/(C+V)**\n- **C/V rises** with mechanisation, so profit rate falls\n- **Counter tendencies:** exploitation, cheaper capital goods\n# Crisis Theory\n- **Over-production and under-consumption**\n- **Disproportionality** between sectors\n- **Periodic crises** worsen over time\n# Reproduction Schemes (Vol. 2)\n- **Department I** (capital goods) and **Department II** (consumer goods)\n- **Simple and expanded reproduction**\n# Other Ideas\n- **Alienation of labour** (from product, process, nature, others)\n- **Theory of rent and money**\n- **Transformation problem** (values to prices of production, Vol. 3)\n- **State** as instrument of ruling class\n- **Revolution** by the proletariat and **dictatorship of the proletariat**\n- **Withering away of the state** under communism, \"from each according to ability, to each according to need\"\n# Criticism\n- **Labour theory of value** is defective, ignores utility and capital\n- **Transformation problem** is unsolved\n- **Predictions failed:** wages rose, middle class grew, no collapse in advanced countries\n- **Neglects entrepreneur,** technology and risk\n- **Over-emphasis** on economic factors\n- **Communist experiments** failed (USSR collapse)\n# Contributions and Influence\n- **Critique of capitalism,** analysis of class and exploitation\n- **Basis of socialist and communist movements** (Lenin, Mao)\n- **Influence on economic history,** sociology, development theory\n- **Keynes, Schumpeter** acknowledged insights on cycles\n> Conclusion: Marx's predictions failed in part, but his analysis of capital, class and crisis remains influential."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit I - Ancient and Indian", "q": "Describe the economic ideas of Kautilya.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Kautilya (Chanakya, Vishnugupta):** minister of Chandragupta Maurya, 4th century BC\n- **Work:** **Arthashastra,** 15 books (adhikaranas), 150 chapters, 180 topics\n- **Artha:** wealth, livelihood and **earth** inhabited by people\n- **Arthashastra:** science of acquiring and protecting the earth\n- **Four vidyas:** Anvikshiki (philosophy), Trayi (Vedas), **Varta (economics)**, Dandaniti (politics)\n- **Varta:** agriculture, cattle rearing, trade\n# Role of the State in Economy\n- **State actively controls** economic life\n- **King's duty:** welfare of subjects (\"in happiness of the subjects lies the king's happiness\")\n- **State owns** mines, forests, large industries\n- **Wealth is the root** of dharma and kama\n- **Superintendents (Adhyakshas)** for each department: agriculture (Sitadhyaksha), trade (Panyadhyaksha), mining (Akaradhyaksha), treasury, weights and measures, forests, ports, textile, shipping\n# Agriculture\n- **Crown lands (Sita)** and private lands\n- **Irrigation:** state-run, water tax\n- **Settlement of new villages,** exemption of tax for new settlers\n- **Protection** of farmers, famine relief\n- **Cattle and forest** management\n# Trade, Industry and Prices\n- **Internal trade:** markets regulated, **weights and measures** standardised\n- **Fair prices,** profit limits (5 percent for local goods, 10 percent for foreign goods)\n- **Punishment for adulteration,** hoarding\n- **Guilds (Shrenis)** recognised\n- **Foreign trade:** protection of merchants, trade routes, duties on imports and exports\n- **State-owned industries:** mines, salt, weaving, armaments\n- **Wages:** fixed by state, fair wages, payment on time\n- **Labour:** protection of women and slaves, minimum wage principle\n# Public Finance\n- **Kosha (treasury)** is the most important limb; army depends on treasury\n- **Land revenue:** one-sixth share (bhaga) of produce\n- **Other taxes:** customs, sales tax, tolls, licence fees, fines\n- **Principle of taxation:** king should collect like a bee collects honey, without hurting the flower; **tax according to ability**\n- **Emergency taxes** in war or crisis\n- **Expenditure:** army, officials, public works, welfare\n- **Budget:** accounts and audit by officials\n# Administration and Law\n- **Saptanga:** swami, amatya, janapada, durga, kosha, danda, mitra\n- **Espionage system** to prevent corruption\n- **Corruption control:** 40 ways of embezzlement listed\n- **Mandala theory:** foreign policy among neighbours\n- **Shadgunya:** six measures (sandhi, vigraha, asana, yana, samshraya, dvaidhibhava)\n# Welfare State Ideas\n- **Famine relief,** storage of grain\n- **Care for orphans,** aged, widows, disabled\n- **Social security,** protection of consumers\n# Evaluation\n- **Merits:** comprehensive, realistic, early mixed economy ideas, public finance, welfare\n- **Demerits:** state control is heavy, harsh punishments, slavery accepted, caste elements\n> Conclusion: Kautilya's Arthashastra is the earliest systematic treatise on economics and governance, and many ideas are still relevant."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Explain the economic ideas of M. K. Gandhi.", "y": "2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **M. K. Gandhi (1869-1948):** Father of the Nation, moral and spiritual leader\n- **Works:** Hind Swaraj (1909), Constructive Programme, Young India, Harijan, Navajivan\n- **Influences:** Ruskin (Unto This Last), Tolstoy, Thoreau, Gita, Jainism\n- **Approach:** ethical economics, \"Economics that hurts the moral well-being is immoral\"\n# Basic Principles\n- **Truth and non-violence (ahimsa)** as the base\n- **Man comes before money** (human-centred)\n- **Simple living and high thinking**\n- **Aparigraha (non-possession)** and limitation of wants\n- **\"Earth provides enough for everyone's need, not for everyone's greed\"**\n- **Dignity of labour**\n# Swadeshi\n- **Use local goods,** serve the nearest neighbours\n- **Boycott of foreign cloth,** Khadi as a symbol\n- **Self-reliance** of village and nation\n- **Charkha** as source of employment and self-respect\n# Decentralisation and Village Economy\n- **Village republics (Gram Swaraj):** self-sufficient, with panchayats\n- **Cottage and village industries:** All India Village Industries Association (1934)\n- **Production by masses,** not mass production\n- **Rural employment,** remove poverty and migration\n- **Small units** instead of big concentration\n# Machinery\n- **Not against machines,** but against the craze for machinery\n- **Machines should help,** not displace labour\n- **Opposition to heavy industry** in a labour-surplus country\n# Trusteeship\n- **Rich are trustees** of wealth for the society\n- **Wealth belongs to society,** not to individual\n- **Non-violent way** of reducing inequality\n- **Voluntary renunciation** and fair wages, not confiscation\n- **Reply to Marxian class struggle**\n# Bread Labour\n- **Everyone should do manual work** for livelihood\n- **Removes parasitism** and class divisions\n# Sarvodaya\n- **Welfare of all** (not the greatest good of the greatest number)\n- **Basis:** Ruskin's Unto This Last\n# Education and Social Ideas\n- **Nai Talim (1937):** education through craft\n- **Prohibition** and removal of untouchability\n- **Women's role** in economic life\n- **Public finance:** low taxes, simple state, cheap administration, salt tax opposed\n# Agriculture\n- **Cooperative farming,** land to the tiller\n- **Bhoodan** and Gramdan (by Vinoba, Gandhian)\n- **Cow protection,** organic manure\n# Gandhian Plan (1944)\n- **Shriman Narayan's plan:** stress on agriculture and village industries\n# Criticism\n- **Impractical** in modern industrial economy\n- **Underestimates technology** and economies of scale\n- **Trusteeship** depends on voluntary goodwill, no legal force\n- **Cottage industry** cannot meet mass demand and growth needs\n- **Rejection of consumption** conflicts with growth\n- **Neglect of urban and industrial** development\n# Relevance Today\n- **Sustainable development,** climate and ecology concerns\n- **Decentralised planning:** Panchayati Raj (73rd Amendment)\n- **Rural employment:** MGNREGA, khadi and village industries\n- **Self-reliance:** Atmanirbhar Bharat\n- **Inequality** and ethical business\n> Conclusion: Gandhian economics is idealistic but gives a moral and sustainable alternative to unlimited consumption."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "What is Neo-classical economics? Explain its contributions.", "y": "2017, 2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Neo-classical economics:** school of economics from about 1870s to 1930s, after the **marginal revolution**\n- **Main contributors:** **Jevons (England), Menger (Austria), Walras (Switzerland)** and later **Marshall, Clark, Wicksteed, Edgeworth, Pigou, Wicksell, Fisher, Pareto**\n- **Also known as:** marginalist school\n- **Replaced** classical cost-of-production and labour theory of value with utility and margin\n# Basic Features\n- **Marginal analysis:** decisions are taken at the margin\n- **Subjective value:** value depends on utility and scarcity\n- **Rational economic man:** maximises utility or profit\n- **Methodology:** abstract, deductive, mathematical (graphs, calculus)\n- **Equilibrium analysis:** markets tend toward balance of demand and supply\n- **Micro-economic focus:** individual units, prices and allocation\n- **Ceteris paribus** assumption\n- **Perfect competition** as basic model\n- **Laissez-faire:** self-regulating market\n# Major Contributions\n- **Marginal utility theory:** diminishing marginal utility, equi-marginal principle (Jevons, Menger, Walras)\n- **Theory of demand and supply:** price as an outcome of both blades of the scissors (Marshall)\n- **Consumer's surplus** (Marshall)\n- **Elasticity of demand** (Marshall)\n- **Time element:** market period, short and long run\n- **General equilibrium** (Walras, Pareto)\n- **Marginal productivity theory** of distribution (J. B. Clark)\n- **Theory of rent and quasi-rent**\n- **Theory of interest:** Austrian capital theory (Bohm-Bawerk), Fisher's time preference, Wicksell\n- **Quantity theory of money:** Fisher's equation of exchange, Cambridge cash balance\n- **Welfare economics:** Pareto optimality, Pigou's economics of welfare\n- **Theory of the firm:** costs, representative firm, economies of scale\n- **Indifference curve approach** (Edgeworth, Pareto, later Hicks)\n# Austrian School\n- **Menger:** subjective value, opportunity cost (Wieser)\n- **Bohm-Bawerk:** capital and interest, time preference\n# Policy View\n- **Free markets,** minimum state, Say's law\n- **Full employment** as natural result\n- **Sound money,** gold standard\n# Criticism\n- **Unrealistic assumptions:** perfect competition, rationality, perfect knowledge\n- **Ignores unemployment** and business cycles (Keynes)\n- **Static analysis,** ignores dynamics and institutions (Veblen, historical school)\n- **Neglects income distribution**\n- **Mathematics** hides reality\n- **Ignores social and moral** factors\n- **Failure** in the Great Depression\n# Importance\n- **Foundation of micro economics** and modern price theory\n- **Basis for welfare and public finance**\n- **Tools used by Keynesian, monetarist** and modern schools\n> Conclusion: Neo-classical economics built the analytic tools of modern economics, though its limits were exposed by the Great Depression."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "Discuss the contribution of Marshall to economic thought.", "y": "2017, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Alfred Marshall (1842-1924):** Professor at Cambridge, founder of the **Cambridge School**\n- **Works:** **Principles of Economics (1890)**, Economics of Industry (1879, with Mary Paley), Industry and Trade (1919), Money, Credit and Commerce (1923)\n- **Definition:** economics is a study of mankind in the ordinary business of life\n- **Position:** synthesis of classical (cost) and marginal (utility) ideas\n# Methodology\n- **Economics as science and art**\n- **Partial equilibrium analysis:** one market with others constant (ceteris paribus)\n- **Use of mathematics** only in footnotes\n- **Realistic and historical** approach\n- **Economic chivalry** and ethical concern\n# Demand and Utility\n- **Marginal utility** and **diminishing marginal utility**\n- **Equi-marginal principle**\n- **Marginal utility of money** assumed constant\n- **Law of demand** and demand curve\n- **Elasticity of demand** (price, measure)\n- **Consumer's surplus:** difference between willingness to pay and price paid\n# Supply and Cost\n- **Cost of production** and **real cost** (efforts and sacrifices)\n- **Increasing, constant, diminishing returns**\n- **Internal and external economies**\n- **Representative firm**\n- **Law of increasing returns in industry**\n# Price Determination\n- **Demand and supply** as two blades of scissors\n- **Marshallian cross**\n- **Time element:** market period (supply fixed), short run (variable factors), long run (all variable), secular period\n- **Normal price:** long-run equilibrium price\n- **Stability of equilibrium:** Marshallian and Walrasian stability\n# Distribution\n- **Marginal productivity** theory for factors\n- **Wages:** marginal productivity, efficiency wages\n- **Rent:** Ricardian differential rent applied to all factors\n- **Quasi-rent:** short-run surplus on fixed factors\n- **Interest:** reward for waiting (abstinence)\n- **Profit:** reward of entrepreneur, normal profit as supply price of organisation\n# Money\n- **Cambridge cash balance equation:** M = kPY\n- **Quantity theory** with purchasing power\n- **Gold standard,** symmetallism proposal\n# Welfare Ideas\n- **Economic welfare** measured by money\n- **Consumer's surplus** and tax on diminishing returns industries, subsidy to increasing returns industries\n- **Concern for poverty,** social reform\n# Criticism\n- **Partial equilibrium** is unrealistic\n- **Ignores unemployment** and trade cycles (Keynes)\n- **Static,** ignores dynamics\n- **Representative firm** is vague\n- **Constant marginal utility of money** is unreal\n- **Cardinal utility** criticised (Hicks and Allen)\n- **Ignores imperfect competition** (Sraffa, Robinson, Chamberlin)\n# Importance\n- **Principles** dominated economics for about 50 years\n- **Created Cambridge tradition** (Pigou, Keynes, Robertson)\n- **Founder of modern microeconomics** and teaching of economics\n> Conclusion: Marshall synthesised classical and marginal ideas into a durable system that shaped modern economics."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit IV - Modern", "q": "How is Keynesian theory of employment different from the classical theory?", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Classical economists:** Adam Smith, Ricardo, Mill, J. B. Say, Marshall, Pigou\n- **J. M. Keynes:** General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936)\n- **Background:** Great Depression of 1930s, long unemployment, failure of classical remedies\n# Classical Theory of Employment\n- **Say's law:** supply creates its own demand\n- **Full employment** is normal, only temporary or voluntary unemployment\n- **Flexible wages and prices** restore full employment\n- **Saving equals investment** through interest rate\n- **Money is neutral:** only price level changes (quantity theory)\n- **Laissez-faire:** government should not interfere\n- **Balanced budget,** sound finance\n- **Micro approach:** analysis of individual markets\n# Keynesian Theory of Employment\n- **Effective demand** determines output and employment (aggregate demand = aggregate supply)\n- **Under-employment equilibrium** is possible\n- **Involuntary unemployment** exists\n- **Wages and prices are rigid** downward\n- **Consumption function:** MPC less than 1, income determines consumption\n- **Saving and investment** equalised by changes in income (not interest)\n- **Marginal efficiency of capital (MEC)** and interest rate determine investment\n- **Liquidity preference theory:** interest is the price for parting with liquidity\n- **Multiplier:** changes in investment cause larger changes in income\n- **Money matters:** affects output through interest rate\n- **Role of the state:** active fiscal policy, deficit financing, public works\n- **Paradox of thrift:** more saving can reduce income\n- **Short run view:** \"in the long run we are all dead\"\n- **Macro approach:** study of aggregates\n# Main Differences\n- **Say's law:** accepted vs rejected\n- **Employment:** full employment vs under-employment equilibrium\n- **Unemployment:** voluntary vs involuntary\n- **Wage cut:** helps recovery vs reduces demand\n- **Interest rate:** price of saving vs price of liquidity\n- **Saving and investment:** equated by interest vs equated by income\n- **Money:** neutral vs non-neutral\n- **Government role:** none vs active\n- **Analysis:** micro, long run vs macro, short run\n- **Budget:** balanced vs deficit financing in depression\n# Criticism of Keynes\n- **Neglects supply side** and long run\n- **Pigou effect:** real balance effect restores full employment in theory\n- **Rigid wage assumption** is questioned\n- **Ignores inflation** and expectations (monetarists, Friedman)\n- **Deficit financing** causes inflation, crowding out\n- **Stagflation** of the 1970s\n- **Applies mainly to developed economies**\n# Importance\n- **Birth of macro economics**\n- **Basis for government stabilisation policy,** welfare state\n- **Led to national income accounting**\n> Conclusion: Keynes replaced the classical view of automatic full employment with the idea of demand management by the state."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit IV - Modern", "q": "Evaluate contributions of Wicksell and Pigou.", "y": "2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Knut Wicksell (1851-1926):** Swedish economist, founder of the **Stockholm School**\n- **A. C. Pigou (1877-1959):** Cambridge economist, successor of Marshall\n- **Both:** neo-classical economists who extended Marshall and Walras\n# Contributions of Wicksell: Monetary Theory\n- **Interest and Prices (1898)**\n- **Natural rate of interest:** rate at which saving equals investment, equal to marginal productivity of capital\n- **Market (money) rate:** rate set by banks\n- **Cumulative process:** if market rate is below natural rate, investment exceeds saving, prices rise cumulatively\n- **Price stability** needs market rate equal to natural rate\n- **Criticism of quantity theory:** credit and interest matter\n- **Influence on Keynes** (Treatise on Money), Myrdal, Ohlin, Lindahl\n# Contributions of Wicksell: Other Areas\n- **Value, Capital and Rent (1893):** marginal productivity theory\n- **Product exhaustion:** adding-up of factor shares (Euler's theorem)\n- **Wicksell effect:** problem of measuring capital\n- **Wicksell's Lectures on Political Economy (1901-06)**\n- **Public finance (1896):** benefit principle, **unanimity rule**, just taxation\n- **Population theory:** neo-Malthusian, supported birth control\n# Contributions of Pigou: Welfare Economics\n- **Wealth and Welfare (1912), Economics of Welfare (1920)**\n- **Welfare:** national dividend (real income) as measure\n- **Welfare increases** if national dividend rises, distribution becomes more equal, fluctuations reduce\n- **Marginal social and private net product:** divergence due to externalities\n- **Pigouvian tax and subsidy:** to correct externalities\n- **Transfers from rich to poor** raise welfare (diminishing marginal utility of income)\n- **Public finance:** principle of least sacrifice\n# Contributions of Pigou: Other Areas\n- **Theory of Unemployment (1933):** wages and unemployment (classical, wage elasticity)\n- **Industrial Fluctuations (1927):** psychological theory of cycles\n- **Pigou effect (1943):** real balance effect\n- **Cambridge cash balance** equation\n- **Industrial relations,** wage determination\n# Criticism\n- **Wicksell:** natural rate is difficult to measure, no clear role of expectations\n- **Pigou:** assumes cardinal and comparable utility (Robbins)\n- **Pigou:** Coase argued that bargaining can solve externalities\n- **Pigou:** classical view of unemployment criticised by Keynes\n- **Wicksell:** monetary theory incomplete\n# Importance\n- **Wicksell:** links classical and Keynesian monetary theory, started Swedish school\n- **Pigou:** founder of welfare economics and externality theory\n> Conclusion: Both extended neo-classical theory and prepared the ground for modern macro and welfare economics."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Critically estimate J. K. Mehta theory of wantlessness.", "y": "2018, 2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **J. K. Mehta (1900-80):** Indian economist, professor at Allahabad University\n- **Works:** Advanced Economic Theory, Economics of Growth, Studies in Economics, Elementary Economics\n- **Approach:** Eastern (Indian) philosophy, influenced by Gandhi and Buddhist ideas\n- **Contribution:** new definition of economics as science of **wantlessness**\n# Concept of Wantlessness\n- **Economics:** science of **wantlessness,** not of want satisfaction\n- **Wants and pain:** unsatisfied wants cause pain\n- **Wants are unlimited,** satisfaction is never complete\n- **Happiness** comes from reducing wants, not satisfying them\n- **Aim of economic activity:** minimise wants and restore peace\n- **Optimum:** state of wantlessness\n# Western vs Mehta's View\n- **Western (Marshall, Robbins):** maximise satisfaction, increase wants and efforts\n- **Mehta:** minimise wants, reduce efforts\n- **Western:** equi-marginal principle, more is better\n- **Mehta:** self-control, satisfaction by wantlessness\n- **Welfare:** from inner peace and moral growth, not from consumption\n# Main Ideas\n- **Wants and means:** a gap between wants and means causes pain\n- **Priority of moral values**\n- **Consumption:** moderate, ethical\n- **Reduction of wants** is the cheapest way to raise happiness\n- **Equilibrium of a consumer:** where pain from want is minimum\n- **Economic analysis** aimed at a happy and peaceful society\n- **Application to planning:** emphasis on saving and austerity in poor country\n# Merits\n- **Original Indian contribution,** attempt to integrate philosophy and economics\n- **Highlights limits** of consumerism\n- **Relevant for sustainability** and environment\n- **Moral and welfare dimension** of economics\n- **Consistent with Gandhian** thought\n# Criticism\n- **Unrealistic** in a market economy based on rising wants\n- **Reduction of wants** may reduce demand, growth and employment\n- **Hurts incentive** to work, innovate and save\n- **Poor people need more goods,** not fewer wants\n- **Subjective and philosophical,** hard to measure\n- **Confuses positive and normative** economics (Robbins)\n- **Ignores role of wants** as motive of progress\n- **Not applicable** as a basis for government policy\n# Relevance\n- **Sustainable consumption,** minimalism and environmental concerns\n- **Happiness economics** (Bhutan GNH)\n- **Debate on consumerism** and wellbeing\n> Conclusion: Mehta's wantlessness is a thoughtful ethical idea, but it is difficult to use as a guide for development policy."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit II - Classical and Socialist", "q": "Critically examine economic ideas of Saint-Simon. Assess the contribution of Proudhon.", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Utopian socialism:** early socialist thought (Saint-Simon, Fourier, Owen, Proudhon) based on persuasion, not revolution\n- **Name \"utopian\"** given by Marx and Engels\n- **Saint-Simon (1760-1825):** French count and thinker\n- **Proudhon (1809-65):** French printer, journalist, anarchist thinker\n# Saint-Simon: Works\n- **L'Industrie (1816-18),** The Organiser (1819-20)\n- **Systeme Industriel (1821),** Catechisme des Industriels (1823-24)\n- **Nouveau Christianisme (1825)**\n# Saint-Simon: Main Ideas\n- **Industrial society:** rule of producers (workers, farmers, merchants, scientists) over idle classes\n- **Two classes:** industriels (useful) and oisifs (idle: nobles, rentiers, priests)\n- **Role of experts:** technocrats, bankers and scientists should plan the economy\n- **Planned economy** and public works (canals, railways)\n- **Abolition of inheritance:** property to go back to the state\n- **Credit and banks** to guide investment\n- **Slogan:** \"to each according to his capacity, to each capacity according to its works\"\n- **Aim:** improving the condition of the poorest class\n- **New Christianity:** moral base of reform, brotherhood\n- **Stages of history:** progress through science and industry\n- **Saint-Simonians** (Enfantin, Bazard) extended the ideas\n# Saint-Simon: Criticism\n- **Elitist:** rule by experts, no worker participation\n- **Unclear plan** and religious mysticism\n- **Ignores class conflict** between capital and labour\n- **Not practical**\n# Proudhon: Works\n- **What is Property? (1840)**\n- **System of Economic Contradictions (Philosophy of Poverty, 1846)**\n- **General Idea of the Revolution (1851)**\n# Proudhon: Main Ideas\n- **\"Property is theft\"** (property in the sense of unearned income, rent and interest)\n- **\"Property is liberty\"** (small possession by work) in a later work\n- **Mutualism:** exchange based on equal labour value\n- **People's Bank (1849):** free credit, labour notes\n- **Abolition of interest and rent**\n- **Anarchism:** society without state, federation of free communes\n- **Federalism** and decentralised units\n- **Opposed communism** and strong state\n# Proudhon: Criticism\n- **Marx's reply:** Poverty of Philosophy (1847)\n- **Mutualism** is a petty bourgeois view\n- **People's Bank failed**\n- **Contradictory views** on property\n# Contributions\n- **Early critique of capitalism,** exploitation by capital\n- **Ideas of planning,** cooperation, credit\n- **Influence on socialism,** syndicalism, anarchism, cooperative movement\n> Conclusion: Their ideas were visionary but lacked a practical path to change, which Marx called utopian."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Discuss main economic ideas of Nehru and compare Nehruism with Gandhism.", "y": "2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Jawaharlal Nehru (1889-1964):** first Prime Minister of India (1947-64), architect of planning\n- **Works:** Discovery of India, Glimpses of World History, Autobiography\n- **Influences:** Fabian socialism, Marxism, Gandhi\n- **Chairman:** National Planning Committee (1938)\n# Main Economic Ideas\n- **1. Socialistic pattern of society:** democratic socialism (Avadi resolution 1955)\n- **2. Mixed economy:** public and private sectors together\n- **3. Economic planning:** Planning Commission (1950), Five Year Plans\n- **4. Heavy industry:** Second Plan (Mahalanobis model); steel plants (Bhilai, Rourkela, Durgapur)\n- **5. Public sector** on the \"commanding heights\" of the economy\n- **6. Industrial Policy Resolution 1956:** Schedules A, B, C\n- **7. Self-reliance:** import substitution, protection of domestic industry\n- **8. Science and technology:** \"scientific temper,\" IITs, CSIR, atomic energy\n- **9. Dams and irrigation:** \"temples of modern India\" (Bhakra-Nangal)\n- **10. Land reforms:** abolition of zamindari\n- **11. Community development (1952)** and Panchayati Raj (1959)\n- **12. Cooperatives** and village industries (supporting)\n- **13. Social justice:** removal of poverty and inequality\n- **14. Non-alignment:** aid from both blocs\n- **15. Foreign capital:** accepted with regulation\n- **16. Education and health** as national investments\n# Nehru and Gandhi: Differences (Nehruism vs Gandhism)\n- **Industry:** Nehru - large and heavy; Gandhi - cottage and village\n- **Machine:** Nehru welcomed modern technology; Gandhi was cautious\n- **State:** Nehru - strong planning state; Gandhi - decentralised, minimal state\n- **Wants:** Nehru - raise standard of living; Gandhi - limit wants\n- **Socialism:** Nehru - state socialism; Gandhi - trusteeship\n- **Approach:** Nehru - scientific and modern; Gandhi - moral and spiritual\n- **Urbanisation:** Nehru accepted; Gandhi - village centred\n# Similarities\n- **Both wanted** removal of poverty and inequality\n- **Both emphasised** self-reliance\n- **Both cared** for rural welfare, democracy and peace\n# Achievements of Nehru's Model\n- **Industrial base** in steel, power, machine tools\n- **Scientific and technical institutions**\n- **Self-reliance** in many sectors\n- **Stable growth** for planned economy (about 3.5-4 percent)\n# Criticism\n- **Slow growth** (Hindu rate of growth by Raj Krishna)\n- **Licence-permit raj,** inefficiency of public sector\n- **Neglect of agriculture** and primary education\n- **Import substitution** led to high-cost industries\n- **Balance of payments crisis** by 1991\n# Relevance\n- **Reforms of 1991** corrected excesses, but public institutions, science and planning legacy continue\n> Conclusion: Nehru's model built the industrial and scientific base of India, though its controls later needed reform."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "Explain economic ideas of Walras (general equilibrium).", "y": "2017", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Leon Walras (1834-1910):** French economist, professor at Lausanne (Lausanne School)\n- **Works:** **Elements of Pure Economics (1874-77),** Studies in Social Economics, Studies in Applied Economics\n- **One of the founders** of the marginal revolution (with Jevons and Menger)\n- **Followers:** Pareto, Cassel, Hicks, Arrow, Debreu\n# Marginal Utility Theory\n- **Rarete:** scarcity relative to utility (his term for marginal utility)\n- **Value** depends on scarcity and utility\n- **Equi-marginal principle:** ratio of marginal utility to price is equal for all goods\n- **Exchange equilibrium:** ratio of marginal utilities equals price ratio\n# General Equilibrium Model\n- **All markets** in the economy are interdependent\n- **Simultaneous equations** for demand, supply, price\n- **Four types of markets:** products, factor services, capital goods (savings), money\n- **Numeraire:** a good used as the unit of account\n- **Number of equations equals number of unknowns**\n- **Perfect competition,** perfect information, no transaction cost\n- **Equilibrium in exchange:** consumers maximise utility\n- **Equilibrium in production:** firms earn zero profit, price equals average cost\n- **Fixed technical coefficients** (production)\n# Walras' Law\n- **Sum of values of excess demands in all markets is zero**\n- **If n-1 markets are in equilibrium, the nth is also in equilibrium**\n# Tatonnement (Groping)\n- **Auctioneer** calls out prices\n- **Excess demand** raises price, excess supply lowers price\n- **No trade till equilibrium**\n- **Stability** of equilibrium by this process\n# Theory of Capital and Interest\n- **Capital goods** earn net income\n- **Saving and investment** equal in equilibrium\n- **Rate of interest** from the capital market\n# Theory of Money\n- **Demand for money** (cash balance) and quantity of money\n- **Money as a numeraire** and medium\n# Distribution\n- **Marginal productivity theory:** factor prices equal marginal productivity\n- **Wages, rent and interest** determined in factor markets\n# Social Economics\n- **Nationalisation of land:** rent to be taken by the state to finance expenditure (instead of taxes)\n- **Free competition** in other fields\n- **Cooperatives and just distribution**\n# Criticism\n- **Unrealistic assumptions:** perfect competition and information\n- **Static,** no time, uncertainty or expectations\n- **Existence and uniqueness** issues (solved later by Arrow-Debreu)\n- **Tatonnement is imaginary:** actual trades occur at false prices\n- **Ignores money,** role of banks\n- **Over-mathematical,** no practical use\n- **Fixed coefficients** are unrealistic\n# Importance\n- **Foundation of general equilibrium theory,** welfare economics, input-output analysis (Leontief), CGE models\n- **Mathematical economics** developed through him\n> Conclusion: Walras gave the mathematical framework for the study of interdependence in the whole economy."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit IV - Modern", "q": "What is the Cobweb Model? Explain its types.", "y": "2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Cobweb model:** a dynamic model of price and quantity in markets with a **production lag**\n- **Developed by:** Henry Schultz, Ricci, Tinbergen (1930); named by **Nicholas Kaldor (1934)**; Mordecai Ezekiel (1938)\n- **Applies to:** agricultural and livestock markets (wheat, potatoes, hogs), shipbuilding\n- **Idea:** supply responds to the last period's price\n# Assumptions\n- **Production lag:** one period between decision and output\n- **Supply** depends on previous period's price: S(t) = f(P(t-1))\n- **Demand** depends on current price: D(t) = f(P(t))\n- **Perfect competition,** price takers\n- **Naive (static) expectations:** producers expect today's price to continue\n- **Market clears** each period\n- **Perishable goods,** no storage\n[[Equilibrium each period: D(t) = S(t)]]\n# Working\n- **High price today** induces large planting for next period\n- **Next period supply is large,** price falls sharply\n- **Low price** leads to small planting, so later supply is small\n- **Price rises again,** cycle repeats\n- **Diagram** looks like a spider's web\n# Three Types\n- **Convergent (stable) cobweb:** supply curve is steeper (less elastic) than demand, price converges to equilibrium\n- **Divergent (explosive) cobweb:** supply is flatter (more elastic) than demand, fluctuations widen\n- **Continuous (perpetual) cobweb:** slopes of equal magnitude, fluctuations remain constant\n[[Stable if elasticity of supply < elasticity of demand (in absolute value)]]\n# Mathematical Form\n- **Difference equation** of first order\n- **Solution:** P(t) = equilibrium price + (P0 - equilibrium price) x (-b/d)^t\n- **Stability** depends on ratio of slopes of supply and demand\n# Economic Meaning\n- **Explains regular cycles** in agriculture (hog cycle)\n- **Shows importance of expectations**\n- **Highlights the role of time lags**\n# Policy Implications\n- **Price stabilisation:** buffer stock, minimum support price\n- **Forward and futures markets**\n- **Crop insurance,** information on acreage\n- **Government forecasts** of supply and prices\n# Criticism\n- **Naive expectations** unrealistic; farmers learn (adaptive expectations, **Muth's rational expectations 1961**)\n- **Ignores storage and inventory**\n- **Assumes competitive market** and fixed lag\n- **Only partial equilibrium**\n- **Policy of the government** and trade is ignored\n> Conclusion: The cobweb model explains why prices of agricultural goods fluctuate, and why expectations and time lags matter."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "Explain Cobb-Douglas production function.", "y": "2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Cobb-Douglas production function:** developed by **Charles W. Cobb (mathematician)** and **Paul H. Douglas (economist)** in **1928**\n- **Based on:** US manufacturing data, 1899-1922\n- **Earlier idea:** Wicksell and Knut\n- **Use:** shows relation between output and inputs (labour and capital)\n# Mathematical Form\n[[Q = A . L^a . K^b]]\n- **Q:** output, **L:** labour, **K:** capital\n- **A:** technology (efficiency) parameter\n- **a and b:** output elasticities of labour and capital\n- **Linear homogeneous form:** when a + b = 1, then Q = A L^a K^(1-a)\n# Properties\n- **Returns to scale:** a + b = 1 constant, a + b > 1 increasing, a + b < 1 decreasing\n- **Marginal products:** positive and diminishing for each factor\n- **Elasticity of substitution = 1**\n- **Marginal product of labour** MPL = a (Q/L), of capital MPK = b (Q/K)\n- **Isoquants** are convex to origin and never touch axes\n- **Constant factor shares:** share of labour = a, share of capital = b\n- **Euler's theorem:** total output is exhausted when factors are paid their marginal products (if a + b = 1)\n- **Log-linear form:** log Q = log A + a log L + b log K (easy to estimate by regression)\n# Findings of Cobb and Douglas\n- **Labour elasticity about 0.75,** capital about 0.25\n- **Constant returns to scale** for US manufacturing\n# Uses\n- **Estimating factor shares**\n- **Growth accounting:** Solow residual (technical progress)\n- **Returns to scale** measurement\n- **Production planning** and cost minimisation\n- **Macro growth models**\n- **Empirical studies** of industries and countries\n# Criticism\n- **Elasticity of substitution always one** (unrealistic)\n- **Constant factor shares** do not always hold\n- **Assumes perfect competition** and full use of factors\n- **Capital measurement** problem (Cambridge controversy)\n- **Ignores technical change** other than neutral shift in A\n- **Multicollinearity** and aggregation problems\n- **Not suited** for industries with fixed proportions\n# Extensions\n- **CES function (Arrow, Chenery, Minhas, Solow, 1961):** variable elasticity of substitution\n- **Translog function** (Christensen, Jorgenson, Lau)\n> Conclusion: The Cobb-Douglas function is simple and widely used, though its assumptions are restrictive."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Write the outline of economic ideas of R. C. Dutt.", "y": "2017", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Romesh Chunder Dutt (1848-1909):** Indian civil servant (ICS 1871), historian, nationalist economist\n- **President:** Indian National Congress, Lucknow session (1899)\n- **Works:** **Economic History of India (2 vols): Under Early British Rule (1901), In the Victorian Age (1903)**; Famines in India (1900); Open Letters to Lord Curzon (1900); India in the Victorian Age (1904)\n- **Approach:** nationalist, used official statistics\n# Main Economic Ideas\n- **1. Drain of wealth:** wealth of India flowed to Britain without return\n- **2. Home charges:** pensions, interest and salaries paid in England\n- **3. Decline of Indian industries:** handicrafts and cotton industry ruined by British policy\n- **4. De-industrialisation:** workers pushed to agriculture\n- **5. Heavy land revenue:** high and uncertain demand impoverished farmers\n- **6. Famines:** caused by heavy assessment, not only by drought\n- **7. Permanent Settlement:** supported extension of the permanent settlement to protect cultivators\n- **8. Limit on land revenue:** fixing land tax (one-third of produce in his demand, letters to Curzon)\n- **9. Tariff and trade policy:** unfair free trade, one-sided duties, no protection to Indian industries\n- **10. Poverty of India:** a result of foreign rule, over-taxation and wasteful expenditure\n- **11. Excessive military expenditure** and war costs charged to India\n- **12. Public works:** irrigation neglected compared with railways, expensive railway guarantees\n- **13. Salt tax** and burden on the poor\n- **14. Indian participation** in higher services and administration\n- **15. Revival of village industry** and Swadeshi encouraged\n# Economic History\n- **Revealed** the growth and fall of Indian crafts\n- **Gave data** on trade, revenue and expenditure\n- **Described** Mughal and company rule contrast\n# Comparison with Naoroji\n- **Naoroji:** drain theory with estimates\n- **Dutt:** historical proof, emphasis on land revenue, famines, and remedies\n# Contributions\n- **First economic history of British India**\n- **Factual base** to the nationalist critique\n- **Inspired the Swadeshi movement** and later economists\n- **Practical suggestions** for relief of farmers\n# Criticism\n- **Biased against British rule,** ignored benefits (railways, law, administration)\n- **Support for permanent settlement** is questionable\n- **Statistics** limited and sometimes disputed\n- **Cultural and political factors** neglected\n> Conclusion: R. C. Dutt gave a factual base to the economic critique of colonial rule and shaped Indian economic thought."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Pigou Effect.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Meaning:** fall in the price level raises the **real value of wealth**, so spending rises\n- **Given by:** A. C. Pigou (1943), as a reply to Keynes\n- **Also called:** real balance effect (Patinkin)\n# Working\n- In depression, prices and wages fall\n- Real value of **money balances and bonds** rises\n- People feel richer, so **consumption rises**\n- Rise in demand raises output and employment\n- Process continues till **full employment** is restored\n[[Fall in P -> rise in M/P -> rise in C -> rise in Y]]\n# Importance\n- Shows that **under-employment equilibrium is not permanent** if prices are flexible\n- Restores the classical result in theory\n- Supports the role of monetary wealth in consumption\n# Criticism\n- **Wages and prices are rigid** downward\n- **Expectation of further fall** delays spending\n- **Debtors are hurt,** as their real debt rises (Fisher's debt deflation)\n- **Real balance** part held by outside money is small\n- Very slow process, so government action is better\n> Conclusion: The Pigou effect is correct in theory, but weak in practice."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Demonstration Effect.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Meaning:** people **imitate the consumption pattern** of richer groups or countries\n- **Given by:** James Duesenberry (1949); the term was also used by Nurkse for international comparison\n# Features\n- Consumption depends on **neighbours' consumption**\n- Lower income groups copy higher groups\n- Developing countries copy rich countries\n- Spread by media, advertising, travel and trade\n# Effects\n- **Raises propensity to consume,** lowers saving\n- **Reduces capital formation** and investment\n- Raises import of luxury goods, pressure on **balance of payments**\n- **Explains why APC is constant** in the long run\n- Social envy and inequality\n# Policy\n- Tax on luxury goods, encouraging saving, local products\n# Criticism\n- Difficult to measure\n- Underestimates rational choice\n- Not all imitation is harmful (improves standards)\n> Conclusion: Demonstration effect shows that social influence shapes consumption, especially in developing countries."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Selling Costs.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Meaning:** expenses made to **create or raise demand** for a product\n- **Concept of:** E. H. Chamberlin, in monopolistic competition\n- **Examples:** advertising, salesmen, samples, display, discounts, free gifts\n# Production Cost vs Selling Cost\n- **Production cost:** meet an existing demand\n- **Selling cost:** change (shift) the demand\n- Production cost raises output, selling cost raises sales\n# Effects on Demand\n- **Shift demand curve to the right**\n- Make demand **less elastic** (brand loyalty)\n- Product differentiation is increased\n# Selling Cost Curve\n- **U-shaped average selling cost curve**\n- Initially cost per unit falls, then rises\n# Equilibrium\n- Firm spends on selling until **marginal selling cost = marginal revenue** from extra sales\n[[Total cost = Production cost + Selling cost]]\n- Optimum selling expenditure maximises profit\n# Criticism\n- Wasteful in many cases, raises price\n- Can create entry barriers\n- Informational value (advert tells about product)\n> Conclusion: Selling costs are special to imperfect competition, and they have both useful and wasteful sides."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Reaction Curve.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Meaning:** shows how a firm changes its **best output** as the rival's output changes\n- **Used in:** Cournot duopoly model, also Stackelberg model\n- **Concept:** interdependence of firms in oligopoly\n# Features\n- **Reaction curve of A (RA):** A's profit-maximising output for each output of B\n- **Reaction curve of B (RB)** is similarly drawn\n- It is **downward sloping** in the Cournot model (outputs are strategic substitutes)\n- Rise in B's output leads A to cut output\n# Derivation\n- Take iso-profit curves of the firm for each output of the rival\n- Join the **peak points** of the iso-profit curves\n- That gives the reaction curve\n# Equilibrium\n- **Intersection of RA and RB** gives **Cournot (Nash) equilibrium**\n[[RA = RB at equilibrium]]\n- Neither firm wants to change its output\n# Stability\n- Equilibrium is stable if RA is steeper than RB\n# Types\n- **Cournot:** downward sloping reaction curves (quantity competition)\n- **Bertrand:** upward sloping reaction curves (price competition)\n# Importance\n- Basic tool of oligopoly and game theory\n> Conclusion: Reaction curves help to understand equilibrium in mutual dependence."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Distinguish between internal and international trade. Explain the absolute advantage theory of trade.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Internal (domestic) trade:** trade between regions of the same country\n- **International trade:** trade between different countries\n- **Classical view (Ricardo, Mill):** a separate theory is needed for international trade\n- **Ohlin's view:** no fundamental difference, only a difference of degree\n# Differences\n- **Mobility of factors:** high inside the country, low between countries\n- **Currency:** one currency inside; different currencies between countries, so exchange rate problem\n- **Trade barriers:** tariffs, quotas, licences between countries\n- **Government policy:** different laws and taxes in each country\n- **Language, culture, customs:** different\n- **Markets:** homogeneous market inside, segmented markets outside\n- **Risk:** higher in foreign trade (political, exchange)\n- **Transport and documentation cost:** higher in foreign trade\n- **Law of cost:** comparative cost theory applies more in foreign trade because factors are immobile\n- **Balance of payments:** arises only in foreign trade\n- **Cost differences:** large between countries\n- **Economic and political independence** of countries\n# Similarities\n- Both based on division of labour\n- Both aim at gains from exchange\n- Same basic laws of demand and supply\n# Absolute Advantage Theory (Adam Smith, 1776)\n- **Idea:** country should produce what it can produce at **lower absolute cost** and import the rest\n- **Division of labour** limited by the extent of the market\n- **Assumptions:** two countries, two goods, labour the only factor, constant cost, free trade, no transport cost\n- **Example:** India makes cloth in 10 hours and wheat in 20; UK makes cloth in 20 and wheat in 10\n- India should specialise in cloth, UK in wheat\n- Both gain by trade\n- **World output rises**\n# Criticism of Absolute Advantage\n- Cannot explain trade where one country has absolute advantage in all goods (solved by Ricardo)\n- Labour theory of value is unrealistic\n- Ignores demand side and cost of transport\n- Two-country, two-good model\n# Merits\n- Basis of free trade and division of labour doctrine\n- Opposed mercantilist ideas\n> Conclusion: International trade differs from internal trade in degree due to barriers, and Smith's theory explains only a limited part of it."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Explain the Heckscher-Ohlin theory of international trade.", "y": "2018, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Eli Heckscher** (1919) and **Bertil Ohlin** (Interregional and International Trade, 1933)\n- **Also called:** factor proportions or factor endowment theory\n- **Improvement on Ricardo:** explains why comparative costs differ\n# Main Idea\n- **Trade arises due to differences in factor endowments** among countries\n- **Country exports** goods that use its **abundant factor** intensively\n- **Country imports** goods that use its **scarce factor** intensively\n# Assumptions\n- Two countries, two goods, two factors (labour and capital) (2x2x2)\n- Same technology everywhere\n- Perfect competition, full employment\n- Constant returns to scale\n- Goods differ in factor intensity (no intensity reversal)\n- Factors mobile inside, immobile between countries\n- Identical tastes\n- No transport cost and no tariffs\n# Explanation\n- **Capital-abundant country (USA):** capital is cheap, exports capital-intensive goods\n- **Labour-abundant country (India):** labour is cheap, exports labour-intensive goods\n[[Factor endowment -> Factor price -> Commodity price -> Trade]]\n- **Two ways to define abundance:** physical (K/L ratio) and price (wage-rent ratio)\n# Factor Price Equalisation Theorem\n- **Samuelson:** free trade tends to equalise **relative and absolute factor prices** among countries\n- Trade is a substitute for factor movement\n# Related Theorems\n- **Stolper-Samuelson:** rise in price of a good raises the return of the factor used intensively\n- **Rybczynski:** rise in a factor's supply raises output of the good using it intensively\n# Leontief Paradox (1953)\n- US exports were **less capital-intensive** than imports\n- Against the expectation\n- **Explanations:** US labour is more productive, demand differences, natural resources, factor intensity reversal, trade barriers\n# Merits\n- Explains the **cause** of comparative advantage\n- General equilibrium approach\n- Explains effect on factor prices and income distribution\n# Criticism\n- **Same technology** is unrealistic\n- **Demand differences** ignored\n- **Economies of scale** and imperfect competition ignored (Krugman)\n- **Intra-industry trade** not explained\n- Leontief paradox\n- Static, ignores dynamic factors (technological gap, product cycle)\n- **Assumes factor immobility**\n> Conclusion: H-O theory explains the basic pattern of trade, but needs support from newer trade theories."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "What are the causes of disequilibrium in Balance of Payments? Explain measures to correct it.", "y": "2016, 2019, 2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **BOP disequilibrium:** autonomous receipts and payments are not equal\n- **Deficit:** payments more than receipts. **Surplus:** receipts more than payments\n- **Concern:** mostly deficit, particularly for developing countries\n# Causes of BOP Disequilibrium\n- **Cyclical:** boom or recession in the country or in trading partners\n- **Structural:** change in demand or supply pattern, loss of export markets, supply bottlenecks\n- **Secular (long-term):** rising import needs in development, falling exports\n- **Inflation:** domestic prices rise faster than abroad, exports fall\n- **Development needs:** import of capital goods, technology, oil\n- **Population pressure:** food and consumption imports\n- **Demonstration effect:** import of luxury goods\n- **Low export competitiveness:** poor quality, high cost\n- **Capital flows:** capital flight, withdrawal of foreign funds\n- **Political instability,** war, sanctions\n- **Natural calamities,** crop failure\n- **Overvalued exchange rate**\n- **Foreign debt burden,** interest payments\n- **Global shocks:** oil prices, crisis\n# Measures to Correct Disequilibrium\n# Monetary measures\n- **Credit control:** raise bank rate, CRR, to reduce inflation and imports\n- **Tight monetary policy** reduces demand\n# Fiscal measures\n- **Reduce government expenditure,** raise taxes\n- **Export incentives,** tax rebates\n# Exchange rate measures\n- **Devaluation or depreciation** to raise exports\n- **Multiple exchange rates**\n# Trade measures\n- **Export promotion:** subsidies, EPZ, SEZ, duty drawback\n- **Import control:** tariffs, quotas, licences\n- **Import substitution**\n# Direct controls\n- **Exchange control** and rationing of foreign exchange\n# Others\n- **Foreign loans and aid,** IMF assistance\n- **Attract FDI and NRI deposits**\n- **Use of reserves**\n- **Increase productivity,** quality of exports\n# Evaluation of Measures\n- Deflation hurts growth and employment\n- Devaluation depends on elasticities\n- Import control invites retaliation\n- Exchange control creates black market\n- Loans increase debt\n# India's Experience\n- 1991 crisis: devaluation, loan from IMF, trade reforms, FDI liberalisation\n> Conclusion: A mix of expenditure-reducing and expenditure-switching policies, with structural reforms, gives lasting correction."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Discuss the merits and demerits of floating exchange rate system.", "y": "2022", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Floating exchange rate:** rate determined by **market demand and supply** of currencies\n- **Clean float:** no intervention by the central bank\n- **Dirty (managed) float:** central bank intervenes sometimes\n- **System after 1973,** end of Bretton Woods\n# Determination of the Rate\n[[Equilibrium: Demand for forex = Supply of forex]]\n- **Demand for forex:** imports, foreign investment abroad, remittances\n- **Supply of forex:** exports, foreign investment inflow, remittances\n- **Currency appreciates** when demand for it rises\n- **Currency depreciates** when its supply rises\n# Merits\n- **Automatic adjustment** of BOP deficits\n- **No need for large foreign exchange reserves**\n- **Independent monetary and fiscal policy**\n- **Shock absorber** against external events\n- **No black market** or exchange control\n- **Efficient allocation** of resources by market signals\n- **Free from political pressure** to devalue\n- **Reduces speculative attacks** on a fixed peg\n# Demerits\n- **Uncertainty** hurts trade and investment\n- **Speculation** and large volatility\n- **May cause inflation** because depreciation raises import prices\n- **Reduces foreign investment**\n- **Hot money** flows\n- **Hurts small countries** with thin forex markets\n- **Competitive depreciation** (currency wars)\n- **Cost of hedging** for businesses\n- **Overshooting** of exchange rates\n# Mundell's Trilemma\n- Floating rate allows an independent monetary policy plus free capital flow\n# India\n- **Managed floating system** since 1993 (market-determined with RBI intervention)\n- RBI buys or sells dollars to limit volatility\n- Large forex reserves to guard against shocks\n# Suitability\n- Suits large open economies with developed financial markets\n- Needs strong institutions\n> Conclusion: The floating system gives flexibility and policy freedom, but needs managed intervention to avoid excess volatility."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit V - External Sector", "q": "What were the main reforms of the New Economic Policy 1991? Evaluate them.", "y": "2016, 2017, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **New Economic Policy (NEP) 1991:** **LPG reforms** (Liberalisation, Privatisation, Globalisation)\n- **Government:** P. V. Narasimha Rao, **Finance Minister Dr Manmohan Singh**\n- **Type:** stabilisation and structural adjustment\n# Background (Causes)\n- **BOP crisis:** forex reserves fell to about 1 billion dollars (two weeks of imports)\n- **High inflation** (about 17 percent), **fiscal deficit** about 8 percent of GDP\n- **Gulf War** oil price shock, fall in remittances\n- **Poor performance of public sector**\n- **Gold pledged** to raise foreign exchange, IMF loan\n- **Inefficiency** of the licence-permit raj\n# Liberalisation\n- **Industrial licensing abolished** except for few industries\n- **MRTP limits removed**\n- **Public sector reservation cut**\n- **Trade liberalisation:** import licensing removed, **tariffs reduced**\n- **Rupee devalued** (July 1991 by about 18-19 percent), then **market-determined rate (1993)**\n- **Current account convertibility** (1994)\n# Privatisation\n- **Disinvestment** of PSUs\n- **Rangarajan Committee,** Disinvestment Commission (1996)\n- **Entry of private sector** in telecom, aviation, power, banking\n# Globalisation\n- **FDI:** automatic route up to 51 percent, later higher\n- **Foreign technology agreements**\n- **FIIs** allowed (1992)\n- **Joined WTO (1995)**\n- **FEMA 1999** replaced FERA\n# Financial Sector Reforms\n- **Narasimham Committee (1991, 1998)**\n- **SLR and CRR cut,** interest rates deregulated\n- **Private and foreign banks** allowed\n- **Prudential norms:** capital adequacy, NPAs\n- **SEBI** statutory body (1992), capital market reforms\n# Fiscal and Tax Reforms\n- **Chelliah Committee:** lower tax rates, broad base\n- **VAT, service tax,** later **GST (2017)**\n- **FRBM Act 2003**\n- **Reduced subsidy**\n# Evaluation: Merits\n- **Faster growth** (6-7 percent), forex reserves above 600 billion dollars\n- **IT and services boom,** exports rise\n- **Poverty decline,** more consumer choice\n- **Better efficiency** and competition\n# Evaluation: Demerits\n- **Jobless growth,** informal sector\n- **Agriculture neglect,** farmers' distress\n- **Rising inequality,** regional gaps\n- **Weak social sector** spending\n- **Crisis risk** from global integration\n- **MSME stress**\n# Second-generation Reforms\n- Labour, land, power, judiciary, infrastructure, IBC, GST\n> Conclusion: The 1991 reforms transformed the economy, but inclusive and employment-rich growth remains a challenge."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "What is land reform? Discuss the measures and their evaluation.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Land reform:** changes in the ownership, operation and distribution of land to remove inequality and raise productivity\n- **Aim:** social justice and efficiency (**land to the tiller**)\n- **Base:** Article 39(b) and (c), land is a State subject (List II)\n- **Ninth Schedule** protects land laws\n# Objectives\n- **Remove exploitation** by intermediaries\n- **Give security to tenants**\n- **Reduce inequality** in land holding\n- **Increase agricultural productivity**\n- **Increase rural employment** and income\n- **Establish village democracy**\n# Measures\n- **1. Abolition of intermediaries:** zamindari, jagirdari, inamdari ended in the 1950s (UP Zamindari Abolition Act 1950); about 2 crore tenants became owners\n- **2. Tenancy reforms:**\n- **Regulation of rent** (one-fourth to one-fifth of produce)\n- **Security of tenure**\n- **Ownership rights to tenants** (Kerala, West Bengal)\n- **3. Ceiling on land holdings:**\n- First ceiling laws around 1961, national guidelines in 1972\n- **Surplus land distributed** to landless and SC/ST\n- **4. Consolidation of holdings:** Punjab pioneer, reduces fragmentation\n- **5. Bhoodan and Gramdan:** Vinoba Bhave (1951, Pochampalli)\n- **6. Cooperative farming**\n- **7. Land records:** computerisation (DILRMP)\n- **8. Operation Barga (1977, West Bengal):** registration of sharecroppers\n- **9. Kerala Land Reforms Act 1963, 1969**\n# Evaluation: Successes\n- **Zamindari abolished** all over India\n- **Tenancy gains** in West Bengal, Kerala\n- **Reduced the power of landlords**\n- **Land ownership** to millions of tenants\n# Evaluation: Failures\n- **Loopholes in ceiling laws** (benami transfers, exemptions)\n- **Little surplus land** declared and distributed\n- **Tenants evicted,** informal oral tenancy\n- **Poor land records,** litigation\n- **Lack of political will** and administrative apathy\n- **Consolidation incomplete** in many states\n- **Cooperative farming** failed\n# Impact\n- **Equity** improved partly, productivity higher with owners\n- But rural inequality persists\n# Recent Concerns\n- **Land acquisition Act 2013 (RFCTLARR),** land titling, leasing laws, women's land rights\n# Suggestions\n- Better records, legalise tenancy, strong implementation, land banks, women's titles\n> Conclusion: Land reform had partial success; better records and tenancy legalisation are needed."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "Explain the Green Revolution. What are its achievements and limitations?", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Green Revolution:** large increase in food-grain output due to **high yielding varieties (HYV), fertilisers, irrigation and technology**\n- **Started:** 1966-67 in India (after 1965 droughts, PL-480 dependence)\n- **Persons:** **Norman Borlaug** (Mexican wheat), **M. S. Swaminathan** (India), C. Subramaniam, **Father of Green Revolution in India**\n- **Rice:** IR-8 variety; **wheat:** Sonora 64, Lerma Rojo\n# Components of the New Strategy\n- **HYV seeds** (wheat, rice, maize, bajra, jowar)\n- **Chemical fertilisers** and pesticides\n- **Assured irrigation** (tube wells, canals)\n- **Mechanisation:** tractors, threshers\n- **Credit** and institutions\n- **MSP and procurement,** FCI (1965)\n- **Research and extension:** ICAR, agricultural universities (Pantnagar)\n- **Intensive Agricultural District Programme (IADP, 1960),** HYV Programme (1966)\n# Achievements\n- **Foodgrain production:** from about 51 million tonnes (1950-51) to over 300 million tonnes now\n- **Self-sufficiency** in wheat and rice, end of food imports\n- **Buffer stock** and food security\n- **Higher farmers' income** in Punjab, Haryana, Western UP\n- **Rural employment** and agro-industries\n- **Modernisation:** machinery, fertiliser industry\n- **Lower food prices** and better nutrition\n- **Spread effect** to other crops (Operation Flood, Oilseed mission)\n# Limitations\n- **Regional inequality:** only Punjab, Haryana, Western UP, Andhra, Tamil Nadu\n- **Crop inequality:** mostly wheat and rice; pulses, oilseeds, coarse grains neglected\n- **Class inequality:** big farmers gained more, small farmers lagged\n- **Environmental harm:** groundwater depletion, soil salinity, pests, chemical pollution\n- **Dependence** on costly inputs, subsidies\n- **Health issues** from pesticides\n- **Reduced crop diversity**\n- **Rise of debt** among small farmers\n- **Unemployment due to mechanisation**\n# Second Green Revolution\n- **Eastern India:** Bihar, Odisha, West Bengal, Assam\n- **Evergreen revolution (Swaminathan):** sustainable and productive\n- **Bio-technology,** precision farming\n- **Water use efficiency,** micro-irrigation\n- **Organic farming,** conservation of soil\n- **Crop diversification**\n# Suggestions\n- Balanced use of fertilisers, rainfed area focus, research for small farmers\n> Conclusion: Green Revolution was a great success in output, but sustainability and inclusion need attention."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit III - Poverty and Population", "q": "Discuss the problem of unemployment in India. What measures have been taken?", "y": "2017, 2020", "a": "# Introduction\n- **Unemployment:** situation where able and willing persons do not get work at the going wage\n- **India:** large labour force, abundant labour, scarce jobs\n- **Measured by:** NSSO and **PLFS** (Periodic Labour Force Survey) using usual status, weekly status, daily status\n# Types of Unemployment in India\n- **Disguised unemployment:** more people than needed in agriculture (zero marginal productivity)\n- **Open unemployment:** educated and urban\n- **Seasonal unemployment:** agriculture off-season\n- **Structural unemployment:** mismatch of skills and jobs\n- **Cyclical unemployment:** recession\n- **Frictional unemployment:** job switching\n- **Technological unemployment**\n- **Educated unemployment** and underemployment\n# Causes\n- **Rapid growth of population** and labour force\n- **Slow growth of jobs** (jobless growth)\n- **Over-dependence on agriculture**\n- **Slow industrial growth,** weak manufacturing\n- **Defective education system,** skill mismatch\n- **Capital-intensive technology**\n- **Low rate of capital formation**\n- **Decline of cottage industry**\n- **Regional imbalance**\n- **Informal sector** with low-quality jobs\n- **Inadequate social security**\n- **Rigid labour laws** (argued)\n# Effects\n- **Poverty,** inequality\n- **Wastage of human resources**\n- **Social unrest,** crime, brain drain\n- **Low demand** and growth\n- **Mental stress**\n# Measures Taken\n- **MGNREGA 2005:** 100 days guaranteed wage employment\n- **Skill India (2015),** **PMKVY**\n- **Start-up India (2016),** **Stand-up India**\n- **Mudra Yojana (2015)**\n- **PMEGP (2008):** self-employment\n- **NRLM (DAY-NRLM),** DDU-GKY\n- **Make in India,** PLI, MSME support\n- **Digital India** and services\n- **Labour codes**\n- **Infrastructure projects**\n- **Atmanirbhar Bharat Rozgar Yojana**\n# Suggestions\n- **Labour-intensive manufacturing** and MSME growth\n- **Skill development** linked with industry\n- **Education reform** (vocational)\n- **Rural non-farm employment,** agro-processing\n- **Boost to services,** tourism, construction\n- **Population control** and women's participation\n# Evaluation\n- Schemes provide relief, but quality job creation is low\n> Conclusion: Labour-intensive growth with skill development is the lasting solution to unemployment."}];
